WEBVTT

1
00:00:27.711 --> 00:00:32.553
Excuse me. Sorry. I'm sorry.

2
00:00:32.626 --> 00:00:34.353
Excuse me. Coming through.

3
00:00:36.262 --> 00:00:38.196
<i>Tonight, so-called
magician Lionel Shrike</i>

4
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.698
<i>is making another sad
attempt for attention</i>

5
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:44.136
<i>by performing what he calls
an impossible feat.</i>

6
00:00:44.214 --> 00:00:47.613
<i>The safe is made of
the strongest iron.</i>

7
00:00:47.691 --> 00:00:50.816
<i>It can't be sawed through or pried.</i>

8
00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:55.172
<i>Yet, Lionel Shrike thinks
he's able to escape.</i>

9
00:00:55.243 --> 00:00:57.084
Dad! Dad!

10
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.918
Hey, buddy.
You made it, huh?

11
00:01:01.996 --> 00:01:05.395
Now, see that seat there?

12
00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:06.961
It's the best
seat in the house.

13
00:01:07.031 --> 00:01:11.271
Is Lionel Shrike the kind
of magician who can pull this off?

14
00:01:11.347 --> 00:01:14.234
<i>Or is he actually
in over his head?</i>

15
00:01:14.304 --> 00:01:15.906
We'll see about that.

16
00:01:15.982 --> 00:01:18.301
- What's the matter?
- I don't want you to go.

17
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.540
Dylan, come on.
We talked about this.

18
00:01:20.618 --> 00:01:22.936
Remember?
This is what I do.

19
00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:25.493
I got to prove all
those naysayers wrong.

20
00:01:25.573 --> 00:01:28.380
Okay? Besides...

21
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.530
I always keep
something up my sleeve.

22
00:01:31.607 --> 00:01:33.687
Now, got your watch
like your dad's?

23
00:01:34.444 --> 00:01:37.206
- Okay. How many seconds?
- Three hundred.

24
00:01:37.281 --> 00:01:39.963
Three hundred. Maybe a few less.

25
00:01:41.876 --> 00:01:45.195
Mmm.
I love you so much.

26
00:01:46.991 --> 00:01:49.878
Okay. See you later, big man.

27
00:01:49.948 --> 00:01:54.586
Ladies and gentlemen,
Lionel Shrike!

28
00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.148
Will he be able
to hold his breath?

29
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.902
Will he be able to
somehow escape the safe?

30
00:02:05.293 --> 00:02:07.692
Three, two, one...

31
00:02:13.085 --> 00:02:14.529
Two, three,

32
00:02:14.604 --> 00:02:19.161
four, five, six,
seven, eight...

33
00:02:20.038 --> 00:02:27.040
296... 297, 298, 299, 300.

34
00:02:30.508 --> 00:02:33.270
303, 304...

35
00:02:34.384 --> 00:02:36.032
<i>It's five minutes now,</i>

36
00:02:37.021 --> 00:02:41.466
<i>and we have not seen or heard
anything from Lionel Shrike yet.</i>

37
00:02:41.537 --> 00:02:42.697
Move!

38
00:02:44.054 --> 00:02:45.134
Dad!

39
00:02:46.412 --> 00:02:48.889
Dad! Dad!

40
00:02:50.847 --> 00:02:52.211
Let go of me!

41
00:02:53.045 --> 00:02:54.205
Dad!

42
00:02:55.523 --> 00:02:57.364
No! Dad!

43
00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.526
Dad! Let go!

44
00:03:01.677 --> 00:03:02.881
Find him!

45
00:03:23.055 --> 00:03:24.260
<i>The Eye.</i>

46
00:03:25.053 --> 00:03:26.974
<i>It may not lie...</i>

47
00:03:27.051 --> 00:03:30.575
<i>But don't think for a moment
that it can't be lied to.</i>

48
00:03:32.326 --> 00:03:35.167
<i>Seeing is believing.</i>

49
00:03:35.243 --> 00:03:37.164
<i>But is it truth?</i>

50
00:03:37.241 --> 00:03:41.720
<i>People see the Horsemen
as noble Robin Hoods. Are they?</i>

51
00:03:41.797 --> 00:03:44.956
<i>Or are they common thieves?</i>

52
00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:46.874
<i>Depends on your point of view.</i>

53
00:03:47.591 --> 00:03:49.114
<i>Here's what you know.</i>

54
00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:52.747
<i>They robbed a bank in Paris
from a stage in Las Vegas.</i>

55
00:03:52.825 --> 00:03:54.474
<i>They fleeced
an insurance magnate</i>

56
00:03:54.544 --> 00:03:56.112
<i>of hundreds of
millions of dollars</i>

57
00:03:56.182 --> 00:03:58.830
<i>and disappeared from
a roof in New York.</i>

58
00:03:58.899 --> 00:04:03.139
<i>Always showering their
devoted fans with money.</i>

59
00:04:03.215 --> 00:04:05.102
<i>Here's what you don't know.</i>

60
00:04:05.173 --> 00:04:07.299
<i>They left one man behind.</i>

61
00:04:07.371 --> 00:04:10.814
<i>Framed. Holding the bag. Me.</i>

62
00:04:12.406 --> 00:04:14.771
<i>Are you listening, Horsemen?</i>

63
00:04:14.843 --> 00:04:18.003
<i>When you emerge, and you will,</i>

64
00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:22.125
<i>I will be there waiting.</i>

65
00:04:22.236 --> 00:04:27.442
<i>Because mark my words,
you will get what's coming to you.</i>

66
00:04:27.511 --> 00:04:29.875
<i>In ways you can't expect.</i>

67
00:04:30.708 --> 00:04:32.867
<i>But very much deserve.</i>

68
00:04:32.985 --> 00:04:39.783
<i>Because one thing I believe in
is an eye for an eye.</i>

69
00:05:57.141 --> 00:05:58.982
<i>Welcome, Daniel Atlas.</i>

70
00:06:00.098 --> 00:06:01.383
Who am I speaking to?

71
00:06:02.815 --> 00:06:04.861
I asked to see the face
behind all of this.

72
00:06:05.812 --> 00:06:10.211
<i>You've asked many questions.
And registered many complaints.</i>

73
00:06:10.288 --> 00:06:12.209
<i>You can rest assured
that we've heard you.</i>

74
00:06:12.286 --> 00:06:13.860
Have you? Because I don't...

75
00:06:13.884 --> 00:06:15.214
<i>- Trust?</i>
- No.

76
00:06:15.283 --> 00:06:18.011
<i>Your leader?
Your fellow Horsemen?</i>

77
00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.569
<i>Perhaps even us?</i>

78
00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:23.606
I don't like that we've been
living in hiding for a year.

79
00:06:23.675 --> 00:06:25.879
I don't like that every message
I get from Dylan is,

80
00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:28.601
"Wait. Be patient.
The Eye has a plan."

81
00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:31.068
<i>Wait. Be patient.</i>

82
00:06:31.147 --> 00:06:32.352
<i>The Eye has a plan.</i>

83
00:06:32.426 --> 00:06:35.393
This is games
and it's not working for me.

84
00:06:37.061 --> 00:06:40.380
<i>You feel it is you who should be
leading the Horsemen, not Dylan.</i>

85
00:06:42.216 --> 00:06:45.933
<i>If that is what you desire,
stay the course.</i>

86
00:06:46.012 --> 00:06:48.012
<i>Trust that your unique talents</i>

87
00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:51.886
<i>and remarkable mind
will not go unrecognized.</i>

88
00:06:51.966 --> 00:06:54.570
<i>We have a new mission
for the Horsemen.</i>

89
00:06:54.644 --> 00:06:57.724
<i>Dylan has a plan
and will gather you together soon.</i>

90
00:06:58.959 --> 00:07:00.608
<i>Now leave!</i>

91
00:07:14.584 --> 00:07:17.391
...North Carolina,
Florida, Maryland, Maine.

92
00:07:17.461 --> 00:07:19.348
We're talking the entire
eastern seaboard.

93
00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:21.340
I need to see some results.

94
00:07:21.417 --> 00:07:23.224
Oh, hey. Oh, sorry.

95
00:07:23.295 --> 00:07:25.389
Just real quick. Can I have you
for a couple of minutes?

96
00:07:25.413 --> 00:07:27.107
I'm right in the middle
of something, Dylan.

97
00:07:27.131 --> 00:07:29.545
No, I know. It's okay. Hey. Hey,
everybody. Sorry. Sorry about that.

98
00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:32.137
Call the mayor.

99
00:07:32.206 --> 00:07:34.605
Thanks a lot.
Twins, huh? Good.

100
00:07:35.443 --> 00:07:36.852
Is he coming?

101
00:07:36.921 --> 00:07:39.001
Look, I wouldn't have
brought you out of that meeting

102
00:07:39.079 --> 00:07:40.568
unless it was something important.

103
00:07:40.638 --> 00:07:43.002
It's about the Horsemen.

104
00:07:43.075 --> 00:07:46.235
Somethings happening here
and it's happening now.

105
00:07:46.312 --> 00:07:48.994
Message boards.
Elite magic society.

106
00:07:49.109 --> 00:07:51.508
There is chatter going on
about the Horsemen

107
00:07:51.587 --> 00:07:54.826
and something happening with Octa,
the giant software company.

108
00:07:54.943 --> 00:07:55.943
I know what Octa is.

109
00:07:56.022 --> 00:07:57.671
I'm sorry, ma'am,
but two months ago

110
00:07:57.741 --> 00:07:59.707
we sent men to Philadelphia
because he knew...

111
00:07:59.818 --> 00:08:02.103
- And I said that I was wrong.
- He knew that the Horsemen

112
00:08:02.176 --> 00:08:03.830
were going to hit
the Euro-Atlantic Banking Summit.

113
00:08:03.854 --> 00:08:04.909
- I have proof.
- Let him talk.

114
00:08:04.933 --> 00:08:07.138
Thank you.
Communications.

115
00:08:07.211 --> 00:08:11.212
Contact between who I think
might be Daniel Atlas

116
00:08:11.287 --> 00:08:13.969
and who may be Merritt McKinney.

117
00:08:14.044 --> 00:08:17.409
And what do you have?
Emails? Cell phones? Any transcripts?

118
00:08:17.481 --> 00:08:19.641
- Pigeons.
- What?

119
00:08:19.719 --> 00:08:22.003
I'm sorry,
you mean stool pigeons?

120
00:08:22.076 --> 00:08:24.725
No, I mean actual pigeon pigeons.
Like carrier pigeons.

121
00:08:24.794 --> 00:08:27.761
- Okay? Magicians work with pigeons.
- Doves.

122
00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:30.279
Stay with me.

123
00:08:31.387 --> 00:08:33.547
This pigeon, right here...

124
00:08:33.625 --> 00:08:36.228
There's Daniel Atlas.
Same pigeon.

125
00:08:36.302 --> 00:08:37.382
East Village.

126
00:08:37.461 --> 00:08:39.063
Same pigeon, West Village.

127
00:08:39.139 --> 00:08:41.901
42nd Street, pigeon. 65th Street.

128
00:08:41.976 --> 00:08:44.863
All the way up to the
George Washington Bridge

129
00:08:44.933 --> 00:08:49.537
on a sail boat with a guy that looks
just like Merritt McKinney.

130
00:08:49.609 --> 00:08:51.018
And what does this all mean?

131
00:08:51.087 --> 00:08:53.531
I know what it means.
It means nothing. It's bullshit.

132
00:08:53.605 --> 00:08:55.287
- Could you sing a new song?
- No.

133
00:08:55.403 --> 00:08:57.403
How is it that two years ago
you were so close...

134
00:08:57.481 --> 00:09:00.134
You think I went out of my way
to screw up my own investigation?

135
00:09:00.158 --> 00:09:01.413
You think I'm
that big of an idiot?

136
00:09:01.437 --> 00:09:02.971
You know what I think now?
I think you're smart,

137
00:09:02.995 --> 00:09:04.250
but you want everybody else
to think that you're an idiot.

138
00:09:04.274 --> 00:09:06.240
Okay, stop.

139
00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:09.358
Cowan, I know I'm new here,
but as far as I can tell,

140
00:09:09.429 --> 00:09:11.668
people who don't even know you,
think you're an asshole.

141
00:09:12.666 --> 00:09:14.075
- And you?
- Ma'am.

142
00:09:14.144 --> 00:09:18.065
You don't know me, but my first year
in the Academy, you gave a talk.

143
00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:22.539
The "long game" you called it.
You talked about a moral compass.

144
00:09:22.616 --> 00:09:24.820
About how you believed
in yours immutably.

145
00:09:26.652 --> 00:09:31.971
And I thought to myself, "That guy.
One day, I want to be that guy."

146
00:09:33.605 --> 00:09:36.128
And now... A pigeon?

147
00:09:36.202 --> 00:09:38.456
Technically, it was a few pigeons,
but I hear what you're saying.

148
00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.054
Okay. So here's what
I'm going to do.

149
00:09:40.078 --> 00:09:43.079
You want to take a vacation day?
Go on your own? Fine.

150
00:09:43.195 --> 00:09:46.480
But I'm not going to devote resources
to another snipe hunt.

151
00:09:46.552 --> 00:09:49.678
I came to clean this mess up,
not add to it. I'm sorry.

152
00:09:49.749 --> 00:09:53.227
Boss, thank you. I promise you,
it won't be a snipe hunt.

153
00:09:53.305 --> 00:09:55.351
We're gonna get those Horsemen.

154
00:09:55.743 --> 00:09:56.867
She's great.

155
00:09:56.941 --> 00:10:00.101
The problem is when you're
two people at the same time,

156
00:10:00.178 --> 00:10:01.697
one of them is bound
to trip the other.

157
00:10:01.737 --> 00:10:04.942
And when that happens,
I will be there.

158
00:10:05.533 --> 00:10:06.772
Duly noted.

159
00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:29.759
- Oh. Hi!
- Who are you?

160
00:10:29.828 --> 00:10:31.563
- What are you doing in my...
- It's actually a funny story.

161
00:10:31.587 --> 00:10:33.189
How did you get into my apartment?
Whoa!

162
00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:35.550
I think it's funny.
It's not like funny "ha ha."

163
00:10:35.623 --> 00:10:37.749
Or you might not think
that it's funny "ha ha."

164
00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.099
I think it's funny.
I guess it's all kind of relative.

165
00:10:40.178 --> 00:10:42.099
I was actually...
No, that's not...

166
00:10:42.176 --> 00:10:45.734
I made myself in the neighborhood
and now I'm here, and maybe...

167
00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:49.730
Okay.

168
00:10:49.808 --> 00:10:51.063
Seriously, how did you get in here?

169
00:10:51.087 --> 00:10:53.486
Are you some kind of
crazed fan or something?

170
00:10:53.565 --> 00:10:55.486
Oh, my God.
No, no, no, I recognize you.

171
00:10:55.563 --> 00:10:58.723
You did that B-grade "geek magic" thing,
didn't you? You pulled a...

172
00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.054
Pulled a hat
out of the rabbit.

173
00:11:00.078 --> 00:11:01.373
A hat out of the rabbit, that's right.

174
00:11:01.397 --> 00:11:02.532
I know that it didn't work.

175
00:11:02.556 --> 00:11:03.691
No, it was actually
kind of disgusting.

176
00:11:03.715 --> 00:11:05.209
It was way worse for the rabbit.

177
00:11:05.233 --> 00:11:08.632
Well, your drop was obvious
and your body shell is flimsy.

178
00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:12.074
So if you wouldn't mind crawling out
from under my couch and...

179
00:11:14.943 --> 00:11:18.342
Ta-da! Now I'm sitting
over here. Crazy!

180
00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:22.102
By the way, that rabbit thing?
That was eight years ago, okay?

181
00:11:22.176 --> 00:11:23.858
I've gotten so much
better since then.

182
00:11:23.934 --> 00:11:25.264
- Have you?
- Yeah.

183
00:11:25.333 --> 00:11:26.907
Okay, come here.
What is it that you want?

184
00:11:26.931 --> 00:11:28.580
I wanted to meet you.
Everybody, really.

185
00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.252
I want to be a part
of the Eye, okay?

186
00:11:30.328 --> 00:11:33.010
I said it. I do.
And I want to use my magic for good,

187
00:11:33.085 --> 00:11:36.768
like you and Merritt
and Jack especially.

188
00:11:36.841 --> 00:11:38.895
Oh, that's right.
I know that Jack is alive.

189
00:11:38.919 --> 00:11:40.254
I know that he
faked his own death.

190
00:11:40.278 --> 00:11:43.119
I am a huge fan of faking deaths.
That was crazy!

191
00:11:43.235 --> 00:11:44.759
What is this?
A trucker's hitch?

192
00:11:44.833 --> 00:11:46.596
- Yeah.
- It's terrible.

193
00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:48.752
- Embarrassing.
- Okay.

194
00:11:48.829 --> 00:11:51.797
By the way, I am so sorry
that Henley left you.

195
00:11:51.866 --> 00:11:53.674
- Ow! God!
- Sorry.

196
00:11:53.745 --> 00:11:56.348
That's actually a little better.
You know what I heard?

197
00:11:56.422 --> 00:11:57.547
- What?
- About Henley?

198
00:11:57.621 --> 00:12:00.144
I heard that she got tired
of waiting around.

199
00:12:00.218 --> 00:12:02.742
She lost faith
and so she asked for an out,

200
00:12:02.815 --> 00:12:04.703
and the Eye gave it to her.

201
00:12:04.774 --> 00:12:06.012
But who knows, right?

202
00:12:06.092 --> 00:12:08.058
In matters of the heart,
so difficult to tell.

203
00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.608
I mean, maybe she got sick of you.
You ever think about that?

204
00:12:10.688 --> 00:12:12.017
Control issues?

205
00:12:12.086 --> 00:12:13.900
Maybe you just didn't
tie her up tight enough. Ow!

206
00:12:13.924 --> 00:12:15.288
Mmm.
Here's what's going to happen.

207
00:12:15.403 --> 00:12:17.688
You're going to stay
here and stop talking,

208
00:12:17.761 --> 00:12:19.204
and I'm going to
make a phone call,

209
00:12:19.319 --> 00:12:21.638
and in about five minutes,
some people are going...

210
00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:30.316
- It's all in the wrist.
- Uh-huh.

211
00:12:30.388 --> 00:12:33.434
Let the momentum
of the card do the work for you.

212
00:12:33.505 --> 00:12:34.630
Mmm-hmm.

213
00:12:34.704 --> 00:12:36.511
This last one I call the Stall.

214
00:12:39.259 --> 00:12:40.907
Not bad.

215
00:12:40.977 --> 00:12:42.546
Now you want to see
a thing of beauty?

216
00:12:42.616 --> 00:12:44.343
- I do indeed.
- Here you go.

217
00:12:44.414 --> 00:12:46.653
And bingo bango bongo.

218
00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.338
That's good. No, no, no,
it's good to be positive

219
00:12:51.407 --> 00:12:52.930
despite making zero
progress in a year.

220
00:12:53.005 --> 00:12:54.051
Yeah.

221
00:12:54.124 --> 00:12:56.045
Whereas when it
comes to hypnotism,

222
00:12:56.122 --> 00:12:58.566
the student has almost
become the master.

223
00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:03.243
I like your confidence, but you might
say that you have the better teacher.

224
00:13:03.315 --> 00:13:04.395
- Ooh!
- Yeah.

225
00:13:04.474 --> 00:13:05.679
You're right.
You're right.

226
00:13:05.753 --> 00:13:07.991
Your teacher definitely doesn't
know what he's doing.

227
00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.031
By the way, was this
your card yesterday?

228
00:13:10.108 --> 00:13:12.473
- In fact it was.
- I thought so.

229
00:13:12.546 --> 00:13:14.144
That's not bad.

230
00:13:19.219 --> 00:13:21.584
If I can hypnotize Danny
before you hit him with a card,

231
00:13:21.657 --> 00:13:22.986
I get top bunk for a week.

232
00:13:23.055 --> 00:13:24.976
Okay, that's a deal.

233
00:13:25.053 --> 00:13:26.463
- Danny, what's up, man?
- Hey.

234
00:13:26.532 --> 00:13:28.586
Stare at the palm of your hand
and as your eyes change focus,

235
00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:29.939
you will begin to notice...

236
00:13:30.008 --> 00:13:31.063
Everything, because
I'm not hypnotized.

237
00:13:31.087 --> 00:13:34.133
It's not working.
Please don't become him.

238
00:13:34.204 --> 00:13:35.886
I didn't know you guys did party tricks

239
00:13:35.962 --> 00:13:37.697
when there weren't floozies around
to impress.

240
00:13:37.721 --> 00:13:39.959
Uh, I think we stopped
trying to impress floozies

241
00:13:40.038 --> 00:13:42.164
in, like, 1937.

242
00:13:42.276 --> 00:13:44.322
Okay, somebody broke into my apartment,
all right?

243
00:13:44.394 --> 00:13:46.156
Some amateur who knew
everything about me,

244
00:13:46.232 --> 00:13:48.756
everything about Henley leaving,
and everything about us.

245
00:13:48.829 --> 00:13:51.512
- That's her.
- Hey!

246
00:13:51.587 --> 00:13:53.825
- Hi.
- You met Lula.

247
00:13:53.944 --> 00:13:56.422
I've met her.
What is she doing here?

248
00:13:56.502 --> 00:14:01.583
I'm the new Horseman!
I'm the girl Horseman.

249
00:14:01.657 --> 00:14:03.180
Whoo!

250
00:14:03.255 --> 00:14:04.335
Yeah!

251
00:14:05.453 --> 00:14:07.976
Nobody. No excitement.
Jack? Anything?

252
00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.016
Dylan, tell me
what's going on here.

253
00:14:10.088 --> 00:14:14.692
Well, Lula's been on the underground
scene for the last decade,

254
00:14:14.764 --> 00:14:16.412
and I think she
has some real talent,

255
00:14:16.482 --> 00:14:18.482
and I'd like to try her on stage

256
00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:20.686
- to balance out the duo.
- What?

257
00:14:20.758 --> 00:14:22.087
No, are you serious, man?

258
00:14:22.156 --> 00:14:24.210
After Henley left, you said I could
get back on stage again.

259
00:14:24.234 --> 00:14:25.644
Not someone who just showed up.

260
00:14:25.713 --> 00:14:27.554
I told you I'd think about it,
and I have,

261
00:14:27.631 --> 00:14:29.884
and I really need you behind the scenes
with me for a little while longer.

262
00:14:29.908 --> 00:14:32.162
Dylan, come on. I've been
behind the scenes my whole life.

263
00:14:32.186 --> 00:14:35.948
Which is a crime, might I add.
Have you seen that man's face?

264
00:14:36.022 --> 00:14:39.818
Much as I appreciate the addition
of some femininity to the group,

265
00:14:39.898 --> 00:14:42.137
over and above what Atlas provides,

266
00:14:42.216 --> 00:14:44.535
I think the real issue
is we've been in this...

267
00:14:44.654 --> 00:14:46.972
Yeah, the issue is that
we have been rehearsing for months

268
00:14:47.051 --> 00:14:49.734
for something...
We don't even know what it is.

269
00:14:49.808 --> 00:14:51.223
And you're going to
keep working until...

270
00:14:51.247 --> 00:14:53.261
Until we work as a single organism.
I know, I've heard you say that.

271
00:14:53.285 --> 00:14:54.540
The thing is, when you say that,

272
00:14:54.564 --> 00:14:57.041
I think what you're
referring to is us, not you.

273
00:14:57.121 --> 00:14:59.520
Listen, I'm getting my orders
directly from the Eye.

274
00:14:59.599 --> 00:15:01.042
Okay? And then I give them to you.

275
00:15:01.117 --> 00:15:02.692
Now, if you don't like that,
you're welcome to go.

276
00:15:02.716 --> 00:15:05.474
No, I'm not going anywhere. But I'm...
Mmm. I'm taking care of myself.

277
00:15:05.513 --> 00:15:06.887
Guys, can I just
weigh in here really quickly?

278
00:15:06.911 --> 00:15:08.446
Because I think I see
what's going on here.

279
00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:11.311
You guys are this amazing,
tight-knit family unit.

280
00:15:11.387 --> 00:15:13.706
- I'm a new person...
- We are anything but a family.

281
00:15:13.785 --> 00:15:17.830
Okay, well, my mother literally
knifed my father in the neck one time.

282
00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:19.914
So you are actually
a little bit like my family unit.

283
00:15:19.938 --> 00:15:21.177
- Literally?
- But, yeah.

284
00:15:21.257 --> 00:15:23.178
It was an accident,
I think.

285
00:15:23.255 --> 00:15:26.301
Okay. So, does this mean
we're actually going to do something?

286
00:15:26.372 --> 00:15:28.452
Yes.

287
00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:32.968
You've all heard of Octa
and their playboy CEO, Owen Case.

288
00:15:33.045 --> 00:15:35.614
His partner, Walter Mabry,
died about a year ago.

289
00:15:35.683 --> 00:15:37.457
You wanna know what all this
has been leading to?

290
00:15:37.481 --> 00:15:38.527
Yeah.

291
00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.964
Octa's hosting the launch
of the next-gen cell phone.

292
00:15:41.037 --> 00:15:42.526
Once these phones
hit the streets,

293
00:15:42.596 --> 00:15:45.437
they'll siphon their users'
information to the black market.

294
00:15:45.513 --> 00:15:49.195
Meaning Octa's selling privacy
to up their profit.

295
00:15:49.269 --> 00:15:52.190
So, the Eye has decided
to expose them for it.

296
00:15:52.266 --> 00:15:54.346
Our mission
is to hijack the show.

297
00:15:55.183 --> 00:15:57.104
Rehearsal's over, guys.

298
00:15:57.181 --> 00:15:58.863
This is what we've
been waiting for.

299
00:15:58.939 --> 00:16:00.383
Now it's time to get to work.

300
00:16:07.531 --> 00:16:09.770
<i>Welcome, Octa insiders,</i>

301
00:16:09.848 --> 00:16:11.497
<i>bloggers and journalists.</i>

302
00:16:11.567 --> 00:16:14.851
<i>We're delighted that you've joined us
to participate in our special event.</i>

303
00:16:14.923 --> 00:16:18.924
<i>Today marks the beginning of a bright
new journey in social networking.</i>

304
00:16:18.999 --> 00:16:20.648
<i>Our latest product, Octa 8...</i>

305
00:16:20.718 --> 00:16:23.480
Keys, cell phones,
iPads, in the bin.

306
00:16:23.555 --> 00:16:25.442
Thank you, sir.

307
00:16:25.513 --> 00:16:29.957
Can't have you texting,
tweeting, recording, or eating.

308
00:16:30.068 --> 00:16:32.717
Sorry, we will take very good
care of it for you.

309
00:16:33.944 --> 00:16:35.468
Oh, by the way.

310
00:16:35.543 --> 00:16:37.225
Security memo from Case himself.

311
00:16:37.301 --> 00:16:40.780
Couple of surprise guests.
Just follow his lead, yeah?

312
00:16:40.857 --> 00:16:42.779
We're all good.
I'm heading to the control room.

313
00:16:42.855 --> 00:16:44.344
All right. Thanks, Jack.

314
00:16:45.173 --> 00:16:48.810
- Atlas, go.
- No, Dylan, I can't...

315
00:16:48.889 --> 00:16:51.263
- <i>What? What's the problem?</i>
- No, there's too many people...

316
00:16:51.287 --> 00:16:53.606
Atlas, I got Owen landing now.

317
00:16:53.685 --> 00:16:55.094
Okay, I'm going now.

318
00:16:55.163 --> 00:16:56.811
- Stop.
- Oh.

319
00:16:58.160 --> 00:16:59.684
- Okay.
- Thanks.

320
00:16:59.759 --> 00:17:02.043
Pascal, we need you in the kitchen.

321
00:17:20.258 --> 00:17:21.985
Look, the speech is about freedom, okay?

322
00:17:22.056 --> 00:17:23.751
That's what we're trying
to sell here, is freedom.

323
00:17:23.775 --> 00:17:25.189
Which part of that
do you not understand?

324
00:17:25.213 --> 00:17:26.708
- I know, I'm sorry.
- For goodness sake!

325
00:17:26.732 --> 00:17:29.335
Mr. Case, John from legal.
Just need your signature for release.

326
00:17:29.409 --> 00:17:31.057
Thank you so much.

327
00:17:31.127 --> 00:17:33.207
Just get it done
and get it back to me.

328
00:17:40.198 --> 00:17:41.852
Hey there, buddy! Smile!

329
00:17:41.876 --> 00:17:43.718
- Who are you? What the hell?
- Who am I?

330
00:17:43.795 --> 00:17:46.159
It is picture day.
Did Julie not tell you it's picture day?

331
00:17:46.232 --> 00:17:48.046
- Picture day? What are you...
- Company-wide policy.

332
00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:49.844
- Look, I got a job to do.
- I have a job to do, too.

333
00:17:49.868 --> 00:17:51.073
Hold on a second.
What is that?

334
00:17:51.147 --> 00:17:52.242
I'm going to need some backup here.

335
00:17:52.266 --> 00:17:53.441
Who are you calling?
Hey! That's my ID.

336
00:17:53.465 --> 00:17:54.545
Security, my name is...

337
00:17:54.624 --> 00:17:56.158
- Bo Walsh, thank you.
- Hey, I'm Bo Walsh!

338
00:17:56.182 --> 00:17:57.546
Not this guy.
He's not Bo Walsh.

339
00:17:57.621 --> 00:17:58.621
Calm down, please.

340
00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.268
Hey! Hey, what's going on?

341
00:18:00.338 --> 00:18:01.912
This guy just came and
put a flashlight in my eyes.

342
00:18:01.936 --> 00:18:04.141
I'm trying to do my job.
This guy, check his badge.

343
00:18:04.214 --> 00:18:05.892
- Who is this guy?
- I'm Bo Walsh, not him.

344
00:18:05.932 --> 00:18:07.932
It says here,
"Evans Mental Facility.

345
00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:09.489
- Mark D. Stooge."
- Hold on a second.

346
00:18:09.529 --> 00:18:10.584
I've never seen
this before in my life.

347
00:18:10.608 --> 00:18:11.812
I'm sorry, Mr. Walsh.

348
00:18:11.886 --> 00:18:13.181
- Come on, Mr. Stooge.
- Hold on. He's not Mr. Walsh!

349
00:18:13.205 --> 00:18:15.012
- Come on. Let's go.
- What's going on here?

350
00:18:15.083 --> 00:18:16.845
I'm Bo Walsh, not him!

351
00:18:16.921 --> 00:18:21.161
God, you cannot trust anybody
in this city anymore, can you?

352
00:18:21.277 --> 00:18:23.051
Get off me!
Look, I want to make a phone call.

353
00:18:23.075 --> 00:18:25.009
- I want to call my boss!
- Hey, hey! What's going on?

354
00:18:25.033 --> 00:18:26.999
I want to call my boss,
and this jackass...

355
00:18:27.111 --> 00:18:29.045
Hey! Make your phone call.
That's your right, sir.

356
00:18:29.069 --> 00:18:31.274
That's my right.

357
00:18:31.347 --> 00:18:33.188
- What the hell?
- You're crazy. Let's go.

358
00:18:33.265 --> 00:18:34.913
This isn't my...
Where's my phone?

359
00:18:34.983 --> 00:18:36.666
Where's my phone?

360
00:18:36.742 --> 00:18:38.504
Atlas, Stooge has left
the building.

361
00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:39.864
Okay.

362
00:18:39.938 --> 00:18:42.587
The circuit board is ours.
I am leaving backstage.

363
00:18:45.213 --> 00:18:47.293
<i>Ladies and gentlemen, Owen Case.</i>

364
00:18:47.371 --> 00:18:49.974
All right. Lula, show these guys
what you're made of.

365
00:18:56.881 --> 00:18:59.723
I can take over from here.
Clark says you're on break.

366
00:18:59.799 --> 00:19:01.083
What break? Who's Clark?

367
00:19:01.157 --> 00:19:02.805
"Who's Clark?" That's funny.

368
00:19:02.875 --> 00:19:05.240
Imagine if I told Roger that,
he'd freak out.

369
00:19:05.313 --> 00:19:07.234
- Don't do that!
- What?

370
00:19:07.311 --> 00:19:08.641
Who the hell is Roger?

371
00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:10.284
Does Lisa know that
you don't know Roger?

372
00:19:10.308 --> 00:19:11.643
- I don't know who Lisa is.
- Are you kidding again?

373
00:19:11.667 --> 00:19:13.681
- You don't know who Lisa is now?
- No, I don't. Just get away.

374
00:19:13.705 --> 00:19:15.909
- That is a dangerous...
- You're ruining the meat.

375
00:19:15.982 --> 00:19:17.506
Don't make me have to go to Brian!

376
00:19:17.581 --> 00:19:19.155
- I don't know who Brian is!
- Why are you acting like this?

377
00:19:19.179 --> 00:19:20.698
- I'll go straight to him.
- Give me...

378
00:19:21.617 --> 00:19:23.583
Oh, my God!

379
00:19:26.332 --> 00:19:27.980
<i>What are you
doing to me?</i>

380
00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:30.410
- Help me!
- Help her. Go!

381
00:19:31.846 --> 00:19:34.290
Have some water.

382
00:19:34.404 --> 00:19:37.086
I feel from your face
you're freaked by the flow of blood,

383
00:19:37.161 --> 00:19:38.809
which is fully fixable in a snap

384
00:19:38.879 --> 00:19:42.403
by focusing and following
the flow of my words

385
00:19:42.476 --> 00:19:44.761
as you're flowing and floating,

386
00:19:44.833 --> 00:19:47.755
which is why you're focusing
on my commands.

387
00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:49.240
You got this far in life

388
00:19:49.309 --> 00:19:51.912
by listening to one voice,
the voice in your head.

389
00:19:52.026 --> 00:19:53.867
This is that voice.

390
00:19:56.302 --> 00:19:57.746
Look at the light.

391
00:19:57.860 --> 00:20:00.668
Listen to your own voice.

392
00:20:05.053 --> 00:20:07.497
Oh, my God.

393
00:20:10.368 --> 00:20:13.209
Wow. What is that?
Is that a miracle towel?

394
00:20:13.285 --> 00:20:14.967
Thank you so much.
Do you know what?

395
00:20:15.043 --> 00:20:18.487
You have taught me that I'm not
cut out for this kind of work.

396
00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:20.162
I quit!

397
00:20:20.278 --> 00:20:21.960
Get another towel.

398
00:20:22.436 --> 00:20:24.402
Thank you, and goodbye.

399
00:20:24.474 --> 00:20:26.440
Oh, God.

400
00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.795
Welcome everybody!

401
00:20:30.867 --> 00:20:33.789
And now, the moment
that you've all been waiting for...

402
00:20:35.023 --> 00:20:36.989
- Nice work, lady.
- Thanks.

403
00:20:37.061 --> 00:20:40.107
Revolutionized music with Octa 2.

404
00:20:41.896 --> 00:20:44.784
I'm actually nervous,
and I'm not even going onstage.

405
00:20:44.853 --> 00:20:47.058
You know, I've heard
that if you're nervous,

406
00:20:47.131 --> 00:20:50.132
it can be really helpful
to picture each other naked.

407
00:20:50.208 --> 00:20:51.970
It's actually picture
the audience naked.

408
00:20:52.046 --> 00:20:53.729
No, this is new.
This is a new science.

409
00:20:53.804 --> 00:20:55.134
So, I don't know.

410
00:20:55.203 --> 00:20:58.409
- Do you want... We should try it.
- Not the right mood.

411
00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:00.082
Guess that leaves
you and me, old buddy.

412
00:21:00.158 --> 00:21:02.965
What? Picture each other...
No. No, thank you.

413
00:21:03.635 --> 00:21:05.999
I mean, I admit,
from the neck up there are issues.

414
00:21:06.112 --> 00:21:09.192
But from the neck down, the David.

415
00:21:09.269 --> 00:21:10.713
Owen Case.

416
00:21:12.746 --> 00:21:16.189
Good morning, Octa-lites.

417
00:21:17.221 --> 00:21:18.710
Now, I've made the claim...

418
00:21:18.819 --> 00:21:21.548
<i>Now, I've made the claim
that Octa 8 is pure magic.</i>

419
00:21:21.617 --> 00:21:24.663
But the truth is, that's just
one of those things I say...

420
00:21:24.734 --> 00:21:28.132
<i>You know, the truth is,
that's just one of those things I say...</i>

421
00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:30.449
...when in fact
I mean something else.

422
00:21:30.568 --> 00:21:34.728
<i>...when in fact
I mean something else.</i>

423
00:21:34.803 --> 00:21:36.088
What else do I mean?

424
00:21:36.162 --> 00:21:42.437
<i>Well, here to fill you in on some of my
fabulous lies and hypocrisy,</i>

425
00:21:42.516 --> 00:21:46.483
<i>and to perform some of the most dazzling
feats of magic you have ever seen...</i>

426
00:21:46.552 --> 00:21:49.155
- They are the world's...
<i>- greatest magicians.</i>

427
00:21:49.229 --> 00:21:51.991
<i>Here to expose me
for the fraud I truly am.</i>

428
00:21:52.066 --> 00:21:56.386
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,
here are the Horsemen.</i>

429
00:22:10.208 --> 00:22:11.970
Hello, New York!

430
00:22:12.086 --> 00:22:13.973
Thank you so much.

431
00:22:14.044 --> 00:22:15.806
Hey, it's great to be back.

432
00:22:15.882 --> 00:22:19.167
And have you met
our newest Horseman, Lula?

433
00:22:22.396 --> 00:22:26.238
- Feels good, right?
- Pretty good. Pretty good.

434
00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:30.437
Thank you, thank you. Um, okay.

435
00:22:30.508 --> 00:22:32.270
We want to talk to you
about your privacy.

436
00:22:32.346 --> 00:22:34.346
What does your privacy mean to you?

437
00:22:34.424 --> 00:22:37.186
Because apparently to Owen Case,
it means absolutely nothing.

438
00:22:37.261 --> 00:22:39.705
No, actually it means less
than absolutely nothing.

439
00:22:39.818 --> 00:22:42.864
You see, he's mocked your privacy.
He's scorned it.

440
00:22:42.935 --> 00:22:45.972
Right, and we're not just talking about
the things that you already agreed to

441
00:22:46.012 --> 00:22:48.217
when you signed,
probably without reading

442
00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:51.496
the terms and conditions
of Octas 1 through 7.

443
00:22:55.203 --> 00:22:58.044
Dylan, the FBI is here.

444
00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:00.439
All right. Don't worry about it.

445
00:23:00.518 --> 00:23:02.518
All right, go to plan C-4.

446
00:23:03.795 --> 00:23:05.636
Lock the doors.

447
00:23:05.713 --> 00:23:07.315
Hey, boss, I'm glad you're here.

448
00:23:07.391 --> 00:23:08.800
Don't.

449
00:23:09.349 --> 00:23:11.997
Guys, look. We got to move
around, get in through the back.

450
00:23:12.066 --> 00:23:13.351
These Horsemen are tricky, okay?

451
00:23:13.425 --> 00:23:16.505
They got backup plans
on top of backup plans.

452
00:23:16.582 --> 00:23:18.628
Hey, sorry, don't mean to gloat,
but I told you so.

453
00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:19.904
Cut the bullshit, Rhodes.

454
00:23:19.978 --> 00:23:22.138
We got an anonymous
phone call an hour ago.

455
00:23:22.216 --> 00:23:23.898
Confirms everything
I've been telling her.

456
00:23:23.974 --> 00:23:25.029
What are you talking about?

457
00:23:25.053 --> 00:23:26.428
You sent agents
to Detroit and Philadelphia.

458
00:23:26.452 --> 00:23:28.737
You've been crying wolf
so we wouldn't send them here.

459
00:23:28.809 --> 00:23:31.413
- Boss, please.
- Keep working on the doors.

460
00:23:31.527 --> 00:23:35.448
Please tell me that you haven't bought
into Cowan's crazy, paranoid fantasia.

461
00:23:35.523 --> 00:23:37.091
Before we start the show...

462
00:23:37.161 --> 00:23:40.878
We had Owen agree to a few terms
and conditions of our own.

463
00:23:40.957 --> 00:23:43.958
So, everything that he had
once considered private...

464
00:23:45.473 --> 00:23:48.599
That he thinks is really private...

465
00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:52.637
Yeah, everything that he once
considered private is now...

466
00:23:52.706 --> 00:23:54.069
Oh, my.

467
00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:02.856
<i>As the Horsemen like to say,
magic is about controlling perception.</i>

468
00:24:02.935 --> 00:24:06.981
<i>You see them as champions of the truth,
but are they?</i>

469
00:24:07.051 --> 00:24:09.972
<i>Or is that just another
one of their illusions?</i>

470
00:24:10.048 --> 00:24:12.617
- What's going on, Rhodes?
- This is crazy.

471
00:24:12.686 --> 00:24:14.180
We got to get everyone
to the other exits.

472
00:24:14.204 --> 00:24:16.807
- Let's go, to the back.
- No. You stay here.

473
00:24:16.881 --> 00:24:19.086
<i>So, since they clearly
love secrets...</i>

474
00:24:19.159 --> 00:24:21.683
<i>Let's reveal some of theirs.</i>

475
00:24:21.757 --> 00:24:23.484
Danny, everyone, get off the stage.

476
00:24:23.555 --> 00:24:25.601
<i>Abort.</i>

477
00:24:25.673 --> 00:24:28.355
Jack, go with them.
I'll see you at the meet-up point.

478
00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:29.430
Let's go.

479
00:24:29.509 --> 00:24:31.635
<i>Do you recall the death of Jack Wilder?</i>

480
00:24:31.707 --> 00:24:37.151
<i>What if I told you he's not just alive,
but he's actually right here.</i>

481
00:24:43.695 --> 00:24:46.979
<i>And do you know who else is here?
The FBI.</i>

482
00:24:47.051 --> 00:24:49.256
<i>Let's let them in, shall we?</i>

483
00:24:50.728 --> 00:24:52.490
<i>And now, for the big reveal.</i>

484
00:24:52.566 --> 00:24:55.214
Go! Go! To the stage.

485
00:24:55.283 --> 00:24:57.363
<i>There's a fifth Horseman,</i>

486
00:24:57.441 --> 00:25:00.203
<i>and he's the biggest
criminal of them all.</i>

487
00:25:00.318 --> 00:25:02.921
<i>FBI agent, Dylan Rhodes.</i>

488
00:25:04.514 --> 00:25:06.196
- I knew it.
- What?

489
00:25:07.671 --> 00:25:09.349
This is so much deeper
than you know, boss.

490
00:25:09.389 --> 00:25:11.603
You think you're looking at one thing,
but you have no idea.

491
00:25:11.627 --> 00:25:14.070
- Who are you?
- The same man I've always been.

492
00:25:14.144 --> 00:25:15.906
Boys, I'm sorry.

493
00:25:24.534 --> 00:25:26.261
You gotta be kidding me.

494
00:25:26.412 --> 00:25:28.378
We've gotta get to the chute. Go!

495
00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:31.212
- Who are those guys?
- Who cares? Come on!

496
00:25:31.287 --> 00:25:32.492
- Hurry, hurry!
- Keep going!

497
00:25:32.566 --> 00:25:33.621
How the hell could this happen?

498
00:25:33.645 --> 00:25:35.379
I thought Dylan
had everything under control.

499
00:25:35.403 --> 00:25:37.165
Yeah. Apparently he didn't.

500
00:25:37.241 --> 00:25:40.321
Maybe you're the leak, Dan.
Where you been sneaking off to?

501
00:25:40.398 --> 00:25:41.932
Don't you dare for a second
insinuate that I had...

502
00:25:41.956 --> 00:25:45.355
- I'm not insinuating. That's your...
- Let's get to the truck, come on!

503
00:25:45.433 --> 00:25:47.593
- Stay together!
- Move! Move!

504
00:25:47.671 --> 00:25:48.717
Lula, come on!

505
00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:50.711
Coming! Whoa!

506
00:25:53.665 --> 00:25:54.745
Whoa, whoa, whoa!

507
00:25:59.339 --> 00:26:03.021
<i>Sleep. Sleep. Sleep. Sleep.</i>

508
00:26:13.525 --> 00:26:14.968
What the hell is going on?

509
00:26:15.043 --> 00:26:17.521
- Wasn't there supposed to be a truck?
- Where the hell are we?

510
00:26:19.599 --> 00:26:20.837
Come on!

511
00:26:20.917 --> 00:26:22.804
Hey, hey!
Wait, did you mis-run the tube?

512
00:26:22.875 --> 00:26:24.524
No, I put it in the truck.
I know I did!

513
00:26:24.594 --> 00:26:26.208
Because we're really
not in the truck right now.

514
00:26:26.232 --> 00:26:27.607
Somebody must
have moved the tube.

515
00:26:27.631 --> 00:26:29.165
Were we above a Chinese restaurant?
Give me that!

516
00:26:29.189 --> 00:26:31.787
- I'm moving! Relax!
- There's something very wrong.

517
00:26:34.104 --> 00:26:36.309
Okay. Why are we here?

518
00:26:36.382 --> 00:26:38.348
Why am I frickin' starving?

519
00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:40.546
Yeah, I'm weirdly starving, too.

520
00:26:40.618 --> 00:26:42.902
This makes no sense.

521
00:27:06.831 --> 00:27:08.195
Okay, sorry.

522
00:27:09.469 --> 00:27:11.833
At least, and I know this
isn't much consolation,

523
00:27:11.906 --> 00:27:14.635
but apparently
we're now surrounded by Chinese food.

524
00:27:14.704 --> 00:27:16.988
- Right?
- Guys...

525
00:27:17.061 --> 00:27:20.619
I think where we are right now, they
don't refer to it as "Chinese food."

526
00:27:20.698 --> 00:27:23.426
It's just called "food."

527
00:27:27.691 --> 00:27:30.373
Wait, what are you saying?
How is this possible?

528
00:27:32.766 --> 00:27:38.210
I believe in your particular parlance,
the word is "magic."

529
00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:40.798
That guy looks exactly like you.

530
00:27:40.877 --> 00:27:43.923
He looks exactly like you.
Are you seeing this right now?

531
00:27:43.994 --> 00:27:45.154
Chase.

532
00:27:45.233 --> 00:27:49.200
Must confess, it's like what I heard
about meeting the Rolling Stones.

533
00:27:49.269 --> 00:27:51.190
They're a lot shorter in person.

534
00:27:51.267 --> 00:27:54.108
Remember I told you about the guy
who screwed me out of everything?

535
00:27:54.184 --> 00:27:57.469
This is my twin brother, Chase.

536
00:27:57.621 --> 00:27:59.348
Wait, did you do this?

537
00:28:01.657 --> 00:28:05.453
As you can see,
my boss don't mess around.

538
00:28:05.533 --> 00:28:08.215
I do not understand what is
going on right now. At all.

539
00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:10.370
Okay, let me break it
down for you.

540
00:28:10.448 --> 00:28:14.494
You jumped off a rooftop in New York
and you landed in Macau.

541
00:28:14.564 --> 00:28:18.087
AKA the Vegas of China.

542
00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:20.479
And presto-changes.

543
00:28:20.558 --> 00:28:21.718
Don't you just love it?

544
00:28:21.797 --> 00:28:23.876
The "greatest magicians in the world"

545
00:28:23.954 --> 00:28:26.432
are the object
of someone else's magic trick?

546
00:28:26.512 --> 00:28:28.672
Isn't that poetic?

547
00:28:30.108 --> 00:28:34.348
No? You know what,
I'm not gonna let you guys yuck my yum.

548
00:28:34.424 --> 00:28:36.867
Shoo-shoo. Or mu-shu. Whatever.

549
00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:42.937
Oh, and you know what they say,
"What happens in Macau..."

550
00:28:43.015 --> 00:28:46.255
Uh, well, I don't know the rest of it,
'cause it's in Chinese, uh...

551
00:28:46.332 --> 00:28:48.059
Hey, thanks, guys!

552
00:29:07.551 --> 00:29:09.949
<i>Reports are coming in
that the infamous Horsemen</i>

553
00:29:10.068 --> 00:29:12.148
<i>hijacked the Octa product launch today.</i>

554
00:29:12.226 --> 00:29:13.828
<i>The trick was on them, however,</i>

555
00:29:13.944 --> 00:29:17.263
<i>as we learned of the miraculous
resurrection of Jack Wilder.</i>

556
00:29:17.341 --> 00:29:20.387
<i>And the stunning revelation
that FBI Agent Dylan Rhodes</i>

557
00:29:20.458 --> 00:29:22.901
<i>has been working as a mole
within the Bureau.</i>

558
00:29:22.975 --> 00:29:26.340
<i>Questions abound today as an
international manhunt is underway</i>

559
00:29:26.412 --> 00:29:30.129
<i>for Dylan Rhodes and his elusive
and criminal band of illusionists.</i>

560
00:29:31.647 --> 00:29:33.885
Hey, seriously, where are you guys?

561
00:29:33.964 --> 00:29:35.858
I'm at the meetup.
I've been leaving you messages.

562
00:29:35.882 --> 00:29:38.201
I haven't heard from anyone.
Please, call me!

563
00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:39.495
<i>And the stock market</i>

564
00:29:39.519 --> 00:29:42.247
<i>saw big gains and modest
losses on Thursday.</i>

565
00:29:43.675 --> 00:29:45.277
Hey, tell me you're safe.
Where are you?

566
00:29:45.353 --> 00:29:49.673
Right where you left me,
18 months ago, Agent Rhodes.

567
00:29:49.749 --> 00:29:50.908
Thaddeus?

568
00:29:51.027 --> 00:29:52.868
<i>How does it feel
to lose everything?</i>

569
00:29:52.985 --> 00:29:56.906
Your job. Your identity.
Your reason for living.

570
00:29:56.981 --> 00:30:00.107
How does it feel to be out of control,
perhaps for the first time?

571
00:30:02.576 --> 00:30:03.701
What do you want?

572
00:30:03.775 --> 00:30:06.776
You're a fool, Agent Rhodes.
You always have been.

573
00:30:07.651 --> 00:30:09.855
- Where are they?
- I'm not telling you on the phone!

574
00:30:09.928 --> 00:30:10.928
Where are they?

575
00:30:11.007 --> 00:30:15.008
And if you're wondering if I was behind
the Horsemen's recent debacle,

576
00:30:15.083 --> 00:30:16.845
I'll let you work
that out for yourself.

577
00:30:16.921 --> 00:30:19.922
Hey, I burned you once, I'll burn
you again. I'm not playing, Thaddeus.

578
00:30:19.998 --> 00:30:22.681
It's not my game,
Agent Rhodes. It's yours.

579
00:30:22.756 --> 00:30:25.233
You started it when
you locked me in a cell.

580
00:30:25.313 --> 00:30:26.995
<i>This is simply my move.</i>

581
00:30:27.111 --> 00:30:29.077
Next one is yours.

582
00:30:32.386 --> 00:30:33.716
<i>No!</i>

583
00:30:34.544 --> 00:30:36.112
Let go!

584
00:30:36.941 --> 00:30:40.385
Dad! Help me! Dad!

585
00:30:41.537 --> 00:30:43.139
<i>Dad!</i>

586
00:31:00.558 --> 00:31:02.479
It's all about blind spots.

587
00:31:02.556 --> 00:31:07.398
When you're staring straight
at something, and not see it. Why?

588
00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:09.832
Blind spots.

589
00:31:09.908 --> 00:31:12.147
We learned that together,
didn't we, bro?

590
00:31:12.226 --> 00:31:15.067
Did he tell you that
we were partners once?

591
00:31:15.143 --> 00:31:18.303
No? I didn't think so.
The "Mini-McKinneys."

592
00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:20.699
<i>♪ Chasey and a Mer-Bear</i>

593
00:31:20.817 --> 00:31:22.817
<i>♪ Gonna make you scare-bear</i>

594
00:31:22.895 --> 00:31:26.214
<i>♪ Ma-ma-magic McKinneys ♪</i>

595
00:31:26.292 --> 00:31:28.292
Cool.

596
00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:34.973
And then, one day, we were 12
and Virginia von Welsheim

597
00:31:35.043 --> 00:31:37.930
had a very nice party
down the street.

598
00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:40.319
I had a little tum-tum, couldn't go.

599
00:31:40.398 --> 00:31:45.922
Mer-Bear did it on his own, and got
a taste of the solo thing, and uh...

600
00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:50.593
- ...and kicked me to the curb.
- Oh, God.

601
00:31:50.668 --> 00:31:55.146
Chase, you win! I concede.
Just tell me how you did it.

602
00:31:55.223 --> 00:32:01.384
Delivery guy came to your door
about a month ago, little za.

603
00:32:01.537 --> 00:32:02.537
Za?

604
00:32:02.616 --> 00:32:05.537
Pizza, huh?

605
00:32:05.653 --> 00:32:09.972
- Is this apartment d3375?
- Not even close.

606
00:32:14.124 --> 00:32:15.568
Sleep!

607
00:32:15.802 --> 00:32:19.201
Relaxing, floating,
drifting, dreaming...

608
00:32:19.319 --> 00:32:21.319
Falling.

609
00:32:21.397 --> 00:32:23.965
So, what he did,
my little womb-mate,

610
00:32:24.034 --> 00:32:27.955
is he downloaded all
your private information,

611
00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:31.230
and my employer
had all he needed.

612
00:32:31.307 --> 00:32:32.671
Thanks.

613
00:32:32.746 --> 00:32:35.553
How did you know how to find him?

614
00:32:35.623 --> 00:32:38.828
- That's a very good question, Atlas.
- Okay.

615
00:32:56.082 --> 00:32:59.844
Just follow the guards.
They'll take you to my boss.

616
00:33:03.275 --> 00:33:06.992
Been fun playing this cat-and-mouse
with you, broski.

617
00:33:07.111 --> 00:33:08.918
Meow.

618
00:33:10.588 --> 00:33:13.065
Have fun at the Sands.

619
00:33:16.742 --> 00:33:19.663
- I am so, so, so sorry.
- Yeah, you should be.

620
00:33:19.779 --> 00:33:22.938
Don't ever accuse me
of betraying us again, okay?

621
00:33:35.323 --> 00:33:38.051
Do you guys ever think that
the Eye is watching this?

622
00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:40.598
You know, that maybe this
is even what they want?

623
00:33:42.716 --> 00:33:44.318
And at the exact right moment,

624
00:33:44.394 --> 00:33:46.633
they're gonna swoop in,
and we'll be like,

625
00:33:46.712 --> 00:33:48.553
"Yes! Thank God you're here!

626
00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:51.107
Thank God!
Just in the nick of time, Eye!"

627
00:33:51.187 --> 00:33:55.188
And then we'll just celebrate,
and it was all worth it in the end?

628
00:33:59.379 --> 00:34:01.425
I don't even really
think that, either, so...

629
00:34:01.497 --> 00:34:03.418
It's cool.

630
00:34:17.761 --> 00:34:18.840
Ah.

631
00:34:19.799 --> 00:34:20.799
Ta-da!

632
00:34:22.436 --> 00:34:24.516
So happy to be working with you.

633
00:34:24.594 --> 00:34:26.272
Please, come in. Come in.

634
00:34:26.312 --> 00:34:28.438
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

635
00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:29.884
Wait, sorry, how are you
working with us?

636
00:34:29.908 --> 00:34:32.829
Oh, well, as much as a magician
who pulls a rabbit from a hat

637
00:34:32.945 --> 00:34:35.753
is working with that rabbit.

638
00:34:35.822 --> 00:34:39.380
We'll be working together. Yes.
Allow me to introduce myself.

639
00:34:39.459 --> 00:34:41.982
Yeah, you're Walter Mabry.
You died a year ago.

640
00:34:42.056 --> 00:34:43.943
Yes. An idea I got
from you, Mr. Wilder.

641
00:34:44.014 --> 00:34:45.935
Fake your death,
the world puts its guard down.

642
00:34:46.012 --> 00:34:48.012
I'm able to control
quite a few companies,

643
00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:49.614
including my old partner, Owen's,

644
00:34:49.689 --> 00:34:52.814
as a consortium of so-called
anonymous shareholders.

645
00:34:52.885 --> 00:34:56.603
- And that violates how many SEC laws?
- I believe it breaks all of them.

646
00:34:56.682 --> 00:34:58.045
All of them.

647
00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:01.007
You see, you all want an audience.

648
00:35:01.077 --> 00:35:04.521
Need one, desperately.
It's quite sad, really.

649
00:35:04.594 --> 00:35:06.674
Hence, my ability to nab you.

650
00:35:06.752 --> 00:35:09.559
I, on the other hand,
want the opposite of that.

651
00:35:09.629 --> 00:35:14.027
I just want to be, and I am
100 percent off the grid.

652
00:35:14.104 --> 00:35:15.389
You know why?

653
00:35:15.463 --> 00:35:17.986
Yeah, because the grid
is for actual human beings.

654
00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:20.379
No, because in a world
of total surveillance,

655
00:35:20.458 --> 00:35:23.584
the only true freedom
lies in not being seen.

656
00:35:23.695 --> 00:35:25.933
You can't control the grid
from within the grid.

657
00:35:26.012 --> 00:35:27.899
- Follow me.
- Yeah.

658
00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:29.778
- Follow me.
- Okay. All right.

659
00:35:29.848 --> 00:35:32.133
You pulled a hat out of a rabbit.
That was very colorful.

660
00:35:32.206 --> 00:35:35.730
Almost eight and a half years ago.
So, no need to bring that up.

661
00:35:35.802 --> 00:35:38.451
We can't all be held accountable
for our adolescent personas.

662
00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:40.646
Can we, Magicolio?

663
00:35:40.718 --> 00:35:43.036
Magicolio, that was 15 years ago.

664
00:35:43.115 --> 00:35:45.002
Don't get me wrong, I love magic.

665
00:35:45.073 --> 00:35:46.278
Like you, and many others who,

666
00:35:46.352 --> 00:35:49.398
I suppose, were not getting any sex
in high school, I dabbled with it.

667
00:35:49.469 --> 00:35:54.833
But, uh, unlike you, I was able to
transition upward towards actual magic.

668
00:35:54.943 --> 00:35:57.069
Science, you may
have heard it called.

669
00:35:59.579 --> 00:36:03.943
So, would you like to know,
my friends, just how I got you here?

670
00:36:04.014 --> 00:36:05.344
We know how you did it.

671
00:36:05.413 --> 00:36:06.413
No, you don't.

672
00:36:06.492 --> 00:36:09.220
Yeah, you stole our files for the show,
then obviously hypnotized us.

673
00:36:09.289 --> 00:36:10.733
Stop. No. Seriously, stop.

674
00:36:10.807 --> 00:36:13.126
The strobe was a combo
of binaural beats, as well as...

675
00:36:13.205 --> 00:36:15.570
I said stop!

676
00:36:15.643 --> 00:36:18.120
You might not be having fun,
but I am.

677
00:36:18.919 --> 00:36:21.363
You have an unusual way of showing it.

678
00:36:21.996 --> 00:36:22.996
So...

679
00:36:23.795 --> 00:36:25.636
How did I do it?

680
00:36:25.713 --> 00:36:30.270
Easy. You see, magicians like to control
other people's perceptions.

681
00:36:30.348 --> 00:36:31.683
In your heightened state of agitation,

682
00:36:31.707 --> 00:36:34.071
<i>you saw the simplest cues.
Black tube, roof.</i>

683
00:36:34.144 --> 00:36:36.543
<i>And your minds filled in the rest.</i>

684
00:36:36.622 --> 00:36:39.463
<i>But the tube you meant to go down
was 20 feet to your right.</i>

685
00:36:39.539 --> 00:36:41.593
Go on, sit down. There's no one
standing in your way.

686
00:36:41.617 --> 00:36:43.105
Don't mind if I do.

687
00:36:44.973 --> 00:36:47.133
Anyway, once in the tube,
yes, you're right...

688
00:36:47.211 --> 00:36:51.007
<i>Blah, blah, blah, the strobe contained
UV letters pulsing the word "sleep."</i>

689
00:36:53.085 --> 00:36:56.881
<i>And that, combined with the sound,
put you into an instant alpha pattern.</i>

690
00:36:56.961 --> 00:36:59.769
<i>Sleep, sleep, sleep.</i>

691
00:36:59.838 --> 00:37:01.521
And from then on,
it really was just fun.

692
00:37:01.597 --> 00:37:04.961
And I can assure you,
you don't know what happened.

693
00:37:09.669 --> 00:37:12.670
That's really creepy.
Really, really creepy.

694
00:37:15.343 --> 00:37:16.753
That's not cool.

695
00:37:16.861 --> 00:37:18.987
Yep, easy stuff, magic,

696
00:37:19.699 --> 00:37:21.108
if you put your mind to it.

697
00:37:21.177 --> 00:37:23.175
All right, that's enough.
So why are we here, then?

698
00:37:23.215 --> 00:37:25.943
Besides you getting to show off
how adorably clever you are.

699
00:37:26.012 --> 00:37:28.819
Well, to start with,
the man you stole from last year,

700
00:37:28.889 --> 00:37:31.687
turns out I had invested quite a lot
of money in some of his companies,

701
00:37:31.727 --> 00:37:34.932
so a lot of what you
stole was, in fact, mine.

702
00:37:35.003 --> 00:37:37.766
Good news is, it's very easy for you
to pay me back.

703
00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:40.569
I just need your skills.

704
00:37:40.638 --> 00:37:43.525
Owen and I were like the Beatles,
if the Beatles had been...

705
00:37:43.595 --> 00:37:44.641
Elfin?

706
00:37:44.714 --> 00:37:46.043
...geniuses.

707
00:37:46.152 --> 00:37:47.755
Pretty sure the Beatles were geniuses.

708
00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:50.149
And like them, we had a dream.

709
00:37:51.027 --> 00:37:54.710
Could we create actual, genuine magic?

710
00:37:54.784 --> 00:37:58.182
And I'm guessing one of you achieved
that dream, and it wasn't you?

711
00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:01.386
That is what he
would like you to think.

712
00:38:01.457 --> 00:38:06.538
My vision was a perfect blend
of elegance and technology.

713
00:38:06.652 --> 00:38:08.970
But Owen wanted it for himself
so he humiliated me

714
00:38:09.049 --> 00:38:12.209
by exposing my private files
to the board of directors

715
00:38:12.286 --> 00:38:13.765
and convinced them
that I am unstable.

716
00:38:13.804 --> 00:38:15.964
Did you take a picture of him
while he was sleeping?

717
00:38:16.042 --> 00:38:17.457
He had me kicked out of
my own company,

718
00:38:17.481 --> 00:38:19.845
and now he goes around
taking credit for a chip

719
00:38:19.918 --> 00:38:21.681
that's not just the key to one computer,

720
00:38:21.757 --> 00:38:24.758
but every computer
system on the planet.

721
00:38:24.833 --> 00:38:27.913
It can un-encrypt anything,
crack the defense firewall,

722
00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:30.753
manipulate markets,
and spy on anyone.

723
00:38:30.827 --> 00:38:32.626
And now it's being sold
to the highest bidder.

724
00:38:32.666 --> 00:38:34.360
If you're so rich,
why don't you just buy it?

725
00:38:34.384 --> 00:38:35.794
So he gets the money?

726
00:38:35.902 --> 00:38:38.062
And the pleasure? No.

727
00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:41.380
No, no, no. Besides, why would I buy it
when I could have you steal it for me?

728
00:38:41.457 --> 00:38:42.617
Oh!

729
00:38:42.736 --> 00:38:44.710
It's being previewed tomorrow
to the various suitors,

730
00:38:44.734 --> 00:38:48.371
which will include your targets,
a crew lead by a South African gangster.

731
00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:49.938
South African gangster.

732
00:38:50.008 --> 00:38:51.783
Yes. How your team
gets past security, that's up to you.

733
00:38:51.806 --> 00:38:55.046
But once you do, you just need
to inspect it and steal it.

734
00:38:55.123 --> 00:38:56.123
That's it?

735
00:38:56.202 --> 00:38:58.009
Come on, this is
perfect for you, isn't it?

736
00:38:58.080 --> 00:38:59.215
You're magicians and thieves.

737
00:38:59.239 --> 00:39:01.842
What makes you think we would even
consider doing this?

738
00:39:01.916 --> 00:39:05.281
Um, oh, wait, I had a reason.
What was it? Oh, yes.

739
00:39:05.353 --> 00:39:06.967
You see, back home,
you're wanted criminals.

740
00:39:06.991 --> 00:39:10.276
But here, I control the police,
the casinos, the media.

741
00:39:10.348 --> 00:39:13.554
I can give you a new life,
out of hiding.

742
00:39:14.424 --> 00:39:17.027
And if you don't,
I'll have you killed.

743
00:39:19.059 --> 00:39:21.663
You know what?
I'm not stealing a thing for you.

744
00:39:21.777 --> 00:39:25.857
- Not unless Dylan tells me it's okay.
- Really? Dylan is where, exactly?

745
00:39:25.932 --> 00:39:28.819
It doesn't matter. Yeah, I'm with him.
So you can go ahead and kill us

746
00:39:28.889 --> 00:39:30.927
because I'm not gonna
steal anything for you either.

747
00:39:30.967 --> 00:39:34.571
Strikes me that a consensus
seems to be forming, Walter,

748
00:39:34.644 --> 00:39:36.167
and we're calling your bluff.

749
00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:39.926
- Actually, we'll do it.
- What?

750
00:39:39.998 --> 00:39:42.761
We're in Macau. The oldest
magic store in the world is here.

751
00:39:42.835 --> 00:39:46.200
We'll get the supplies
we need, and we'll do it.

752
00:39:46.831 --> 00:39:48.719
Thank you.

753
00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:50.278
It should be fun.

754
00:39:50.348 --> 00:39:53.349
Chase will take you
to the magic shop in the morning.

755
00:39:58.460 --> 00:39:59.460
Badge.

756
00:40:03.175 --> 00:40:04.335
Really?

757
00:40:06.452 --> 00:40:07.861
Thanks.

758
00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:12.375
Agent Cowan. Your ID.

759
00:40:12.486 --> 00:40:13.895
Oh, yeah. Thanks.

760
00:40:17.960 --> 00:40:20.438
It appears Mr. Bradley's
still at dinner.

761
00:40:20.518 --> 00:40:23.246
Yeah? Tell him recess is over.

762
00:40:31.147 --> 00:40:32.147
What?

763
00:40:50.288 --> 00:40:51.652
All right, so I made my move.

764
00:40:51.727 --> 00:40:53.886
Where are they?
I don't have time for bullshit.

765
00:40:53.964 --> 00:40:56.488
Oh, I have all the time
in the world, thanks to you.

766
00:40:56.562 --> 00:40:59.960
And I didn't get to finish my lobster,
which makes me very grumpy.

767
00:41:00.038 --> 00:41:02.800
Okay, let's cut the crap.
What do you want?

768
00:41:02.875 --> 00:41:06.035
You can dispense with
the tough guy act to begin with.

769
00:41:06.152 --> 00:41:08.676
You're too desperate
for it to be believable.

770
00:41:10.788 --> 00:41:13.391
I said, what do you want?

771
00:41:13.465 --> 00:41:17.068
You used a form 219 warrant
to search my room.

772
00:41:17.141 --> 00:41:19.823
You found a connection
between me and the Four Horsemen.

773
00:41:19.898 --> 00:41:25.024
Which means you can take me
out of here on a 24-hour leave.

774
00:41:25.093 --> 00:41:29.299
- You want me to break you out?
- I want you to take me out.

775
00:41:29.369 --> 00:41:31.937
All you need is an extradition order,

776
00:41:32.006 --> 00:41:34.735
which you'll find in
that tray by my printer.

777
00:41:35.603 --> 00:41:40.967
Dylan, I had nothing to do
with the Horsemen's disappearance.

778
00:41:41.037 --> 00:41:43.515
You were lured into a trap.

779
00:41:43.595 --> 00:41:46.641
And like Dorothy did
when she landed in Oz,

780
00:41:46.712 --> 00:41:52.951
you're beginning to realize
that the only way out is through.

781
00:41:53.025 --> 00:41:56.026
And I'm the only one
who can get you there.

782
00:41:56.941 --> 00:41:58.305
You got this place wired.

783
00:41:58.380 --> 00:42:02.506
You don't need me to get out of here.
This is all about payback.

784
00:42:02.576 --> 00:42:06.372
- I believe in an eye for an eye.
- So you're out to destroy me?

785
00:42:06.452 --> 00:42:07.657
Oh, absolutely.

786
00:42:07.731 --> 00:42:09.025
You think I'm still gonna
play your little game?

787
00:42:09.049 --> 00:42:10.732
I know you will.

788
00:42:10.807 --> 00:42:14.808
Because no matter how hard you try
to find a culprit behind all this,

789
00:42:14.883 --> 00:42:17.248
you're going to blame only yourself.

790
00:42:17.361 --> 00:42:21.805
When your little vendetta ended with you
putting me in jail 18 months ago,

791
00:42:21.876 --> 00:42:25.764
you got tired, you got sloppy.
You stopped paying attention.

792
00:42:25.832 --> 00:42:27.878
And that's where you lost.

793
00:42:31.067 --> 00:42:34.546
So, you can save yourself,

794
00:42:36.302 --> 00:42:40.781
or you can make a deal with me,
and save the Four Horsemen,

795
00:42:40.857 --> 00:42:44.142
and stop wasting both of our time.

796
00:42:46.292 --> 00:42:51.737
Steal something for this narcissistic
little man boy? I don't like it.

797
00:42:51.806 --> 00:42:53.932
But what I like less
is you deciding for us.

798
00:42:54.004 --> 00:42:56.618
Yeah? Do you like that we're the
laughing stocks of the magic world,

799
00:42:56.642 --> 00:42:58.483
and the fugitives
of the actual world?

800
00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:00.287
Walter is right.
We have nowhere to go.

801
00:43:00.358 --> 00:43:01.597
But we can fix that.

802
00:43:01.677 --> 00:43:02.881
- We can fix it?
- Yeah.

803
00:43:02.955 --> 00:43:04.530
So you're officially
part of the team now?

804
00:43:04.554 --> 00:43:06.361
- Excuse me?
- I'm sure this is what Dylan

805
00:43:06.432 --> 00:43:08.566
meant when he said we should
all work as a single organism.

806
00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:11.829
Yeah, that is a fairytale
that Dylan tells himself, and tells us.

807
00:43:11.906 --> 00:43:14.555
Look, even if we get this stick,
he'll never let us go.

808
00:43:14.624 --> 00:43:18.705
We can't trust Walter, okay?
The Eye has a history in Macau.

809
00:43:18.819 --> 00:43:20.194
They're the only ones we can trust.

810
00:43:20.218 --> 00:43:21.273
Now, if we can get it to them,

811
00:43:21.297 --> 00:43:23.582
they can clear our names
and get us the hell out of here.

812
00:43:23.695 --> 00:43:25.183
It's a bad plan, man.

813
00:43:32.845 --> 00:43:34.767
Hey.

814
00:43:34.843 --> 00:43:36.128
Hi, how are you?

815
00:43:36.202 --> 00:43:38.168
Um, we need some
things custom-made.

816
00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:39.894
Actually, by tomorrow.
We're kind of in a rush.

817
00:43:39.918 --> 00:43:41.601
And...

818
00:43:41.677 --> 00:43:43.881
You have no idea what
I'm talking about, do you?

819
00:43:49.828 --> 00:43:53.113
Welcome to long's.
My name's Li. Not Bruce Lee.

820
00:43:53.185 --> 00:43:54.424
- What?
- Got you.

821
00:43:54.504 --> 00:43:55.550
- Okay.
- Look.

822
00:43:57.581 --> 00:43:58.581
Where'd it go?

823
00:44:01.217 --> 00:44:03.024
Oh! And now it's by my head. How fun.

824
00:44:03.095 --> 00:44:04.664
- $20.
- $20?

825
00:44:04.734 --> 00:44:06.621
No, thank you.
We're kind of in a rush.

826
00:44:06.692 --> 00:44:08.930
- $10.
- Uh, no.

827
00:44:09.009 --> 00:44:10.294
- Two for one.
- No.

828
00:44:10.368 --> 00:44:11.982
Okay, all right.
Just give us the thumbs. Yeah?

829
00:44:12.006 --> 00:44:14.245
Okay. Thanks a lot, Jack.
Please take these.

830
00:44:14.324 --> 00:44:16.972
So, we need a Keplinger holdout,

831
00:44:17.041 --> 00:44:21.042
and we also need roughing fluid,
a Sanada gimmick, sleeves, tracks.

832
00:44:21.117 --> 00:44:23.038
This is all in plastic,
though, if you can.

833
00:44:27.631 --> 00:44:28.631
What?

834
00:44:30.867 --> 00:44:32.391
Is there a problem?

835
00:44:32.466 --> 00:44:35.626
My grandmother wants to know
why plastic? Plastic is cheap.

836
00:44:35.703 --> 00:44:37.987
Sorry. Yeah, we need plastic
because we're...

837
00:44:38.060 --> 00:44:41.618
We're going through a very sensitive
metal detector, okay?

838
00:44:41.737 --> 00:44:44.419
So plastic, nothing. Metal...

839
00:44:46.532 --> 00:44:48.578
It detects.

840
00:44:53.085 --> 00:44:54.131
What did she say?

841
00:44:54.204 --> 00:44:57.569
She said just because you're
talking slowly and moving your hands

842
00:44:57.641 --> 00:44:59.641
doesn't mean she can suddenly
understand English.

843
00:44:59.719 --> 00:45:02.481
Oh, yeah. So sorry. He's racist.

844
00:45:02.556 --> 00:45:06.762
Hey, bro. Listen, I know
that the only thing that we agree on

845
00:45:06.831 --> 00:45:09.435
is that our mom and dad
had one too many children.

846
00:45:09.509 --> 00:45:13.794
So why don't we just let
the kids play with their toys

847
00:45:13.864 --> 00:45:15.353
and go get a little drinkey-poo?

848
00:45:15.423 --> 00:45:20.788
- If it's free, it's me.
- Great. Guys, we'll be right back.

849
00:45:20.857 --> 00:45:24.461
Okay, kiddies,
the chaperones are watching you.

850
00:45:24.534 --> 00:45:26.818
Don't do anything I wouldn't enjoy.

851
00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:29.936
Okay. Have fun, you two.

852
00:45:32.286 --> 00:45:35.730
Hey. Can I use your phone?
It's kind of important.

853
00:45:35.802 --> 00:45:37.166
Sure.

854
00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:39.649
- On the house.
- Thanks.

855
00:45:41.317 --> 00:45:42.397
Who are you calling?

856
00:45:42.476 --> 00:45:43.970
I'm arranging
to hand over the stick.

857
00:45:43.994 --> 00:45:45.757
So that's how we've been
contacting the Eye?

858
00:45:45.832 --> 00:45:47.117
You've been doing it
on your own?

859
00:45:47.191 --> 00:45:48.316
Maybe.

860
00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:50.789
Oh, God.
Dylan's not gonna like this.

861
00:45:50.867 --> 00:45:52.947
I'm not really concerned
with what Dylan likes. Okay?

862
00:45:53.025 --> 00:45:54.264
I'm doing this for us.

863
00:45:56.422 --> 00:46:00.184
- What is wrong with this thing?
- Hey, relax, it's just a kid's toy.

864
00:46:00.258 --> 00:46:01.588
Yeah, that's what I thought.

865
00:46:01.657 --> 00:46:03.020
- Okay. Let me just...
- Ow!

866
00:46:03.095 --> 00:46:05.460
Sorry, sorry. Jesus.

867
00:46:07.491 --> 00:46:09.934
Hey, can you get this?

868
00:46:14.524 --> 00:46:16.490
It's like birth, you know.

869
00:46:16.562 --> 00:46:20.005
One person enters the hospital,
two people come out.

870
00:46:20.078 --> 00:46:21.522
Walk and talk.

871
00:46:21.597 --> 00:46:23.915
I'll tell you on the plane.

872
00:46:23.994 --> 00:46:26.120
What plane?

873
00:46:26.192 --> 00:46:29.159
My sources tell me
your Horsemen are in Macau.

874
00:46:29.908 --> 00:46:32.193
- What's the look?
- It's none of your business.

875
00:46:32.266 --> 00:46:33.471
Who are your sources?

876
00:46:36.142 --> 00:46:38.222
Just some old friends.

877
00:46:45.053 --> 00:46:48.054
When we have the card, where do I go?

878
00:46:48.130 --> 00:46:50.529
And 0 for 52.

879
00:46:50.608 --> 00:46:53.654
Well, it's perfect.
It's a perfect score.

880
00:46:54.724 --> 00:46:57.565
Then what about girls?

881
00:46:57.641 --> 00:46:59.482
Like, relationships?

882
00:46:59.559 --> 00:47:00.684
What?

883
00:47:04.554 --> 00:47:08.111
Look, it's not
something I'm proud of,

884
00:47:08.190 --> 00:47:14.226
but every girl I get close to,
I end up taking their trust.

885
00:47:14.304 --> 00:47:16.782
And then their wallet.

886
00:47:17.381 --> 00:47:18.506
So...

887
00:47:20.977 --> 00:47:23.501
- You take their wallets?
- Yeah.

888
00:47:25.733 --> 00:47:27.858
You ever take their belts?

889
00:47:29.289 --> 00:47:33.734
Or how about some peanuts
from the hotel bar?

890
00:47:33.804 --> 00:47:36.772
Not sure why you took those.

891
00:47:36.841 --> 00:47:39.921
I'm sorry, did you think
that I was like those other girls?

892
00:47:41.477 --> 00:47:43.284
Oh, Jack.

893
00:47:48.989 --> 00:47:51.194
Okay. So there's a marketplace
down the street.

894
00:47:51.267 --> 00:47:53.393
And I'm meeting
a man with a blue hat.

895
00:47:53.465 --> 00:47:56.193
He'll get the stick to the right people
and get us home.

896
00:47:56.262 --> 00:47:58.308
You go grab Merritt, okay?
We got a lot to do.

897
00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:00.107
And, hey, put your belt on.

898
00:48:00.178 --> 00:48:02.860
Oh, no, it's not...
She took it off me and...

899
00:48:04.094 --> 00:48:06.935
You know, the most
heartbreaking moment of my life

900
00:48:07.011 --> 00:48:11.729
was when I discovered that you had
stolen all my money and split.

901
00:48:11.806 --> 00:48:13.886
I didn't realize until now

902
00:48:13.964 --> 00:48:16.442
how much of that money
you put into your face.

903
00:48:16.522 --> 00:48:21.079
Seriously, is it weird to have more hair
in your nose than on your head?

904
00:48:21.157 --> 00:48:23.726
Speaking of, next time
you get a hair transplant,

905
00:48:23.795 --> 00:48:29.432
you might wanna consider requesting
something other than old man pubes.

906
00:48:29.509 --> 00:48:33.555
You know what?
You are such a buffoon, Mer-Bear.

907
00:48:33.625 --> 00:48:35.546
And this little ploy you have

908
00:48:35.623 --> 00:48:39.704
of stalling me while your friends
screw over my employer?

909
00:48:39.818 --> 00:48:41.421
Not gonna work.

910
00:48:43.814 --> 00:48:48.020
Chase! As you stare into my eyes,
you will find that you wanna sleep.

911
00:48:48.090 --> 00:48:50.739
When you wake, you will beg
for Merritt's forgiveness

912
00:48:50.807 --> 00:48:53.967
and realize that you will never be
half the man that he is.

913
00:48:56.002 --> 00:49:02.686
Merritt, I am so,
so very, very sorry

914
00:49:02.756 --> 00:49:05.484
about how pathetic your pupil is.

915
00:49:05.553 --> 00:49:08.713
Hey, a little hint
on your technique of surprise.

916
00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:13.109
It lacks that all-essential
little element of surprise.

917
00:49:13.625 --> 00:49:17.989
Hey, this has been fun, Mer-Bear.
Let's do it again sometime.

918
00:49:21.417 --> 00:49:26.339
Well, I've had ample time
to do some research, and you know,

919
00:49:26.412 --> 00:49:27.458
I was wrong.

920
00:49:27.531 --> 00:49:31.134
I think the Eye
might actually be real.

921
00:49:31.207 --> 00:49:32.696
Thought you didn't
buy into all that.

922
00:49:32.766 --> 00:49:34.766
I'd be lying if I
said I didn't think

923
00:49:34.843 --> 00:49:38.162
that this road would lead to
the great wizard behind the curtain

924
00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:40.808
and, in the end,
I might get to see his face.

925
00:49:40.877 --> 00:49:42.082
That ain't gonna happen.

926
00:49:42.156 --> 00:49:43.771
Even if the fate of
the Horsemen depends on it?

927
00:49:43.795 --> 00:49:46.528
Listen to me. I broke you out of there.
We're not changing the terms now.

928
00:49:46.552 --> 00:49:49.280
- You put me there!
- Yeah, because you deserve to be there.

929
00:49:49.349 --> 00:49:52.872
- Listen, I did not kill your father.
- What?

930
00:49:52.985 --> 00:49:55.906
All right, all right,
let's just cut the bullshit.

931
00:49:55.982 --> 00:49:59.540
I know who you are, Shrike.

932
00:50:00.897 --> 00:50:02.897
I know you blame me
for your father's death.

933
00:50:02.975 --> 00:50:06.942
I know you think I goaded him into
performing a trick he couldn't pull off.

934
00:50:07.011 --> 00:50:11.853
I know the time in jail was a result
of a 30-year revenge plot against me.

935
00:50:11.926 --> 00:50:14.131
Supposing that's all true,
you think I'd tell you now?

936
00:50:14.204 --> 00:50:15.409
Man...

937
00:50:17.121 --> 00:50:18.770
Thirty years.

938
00:50:18.839 --> 00:50:24.443
Thirty years, and the greatest long game
magic trick ever created.

939
00:50:24.514 --> 00:50:28.310
Just to put me behind bars. Okay.

940
00:50:29.868 --> 00:50:31.756
So how did it feel, seeing me there?

941
00:50:32.786 --> 00:50:34.548
Was it all you hoped?

942
00:50:34.624 --> 00:50:36.118
I don't know what you're talking about.

943
00:50:36.142 --> 00:50:40.223
You're lying.
It was an empty experience.

944
00:50:40.298 --> 00:50:42.344
And you wanna know how I know?

945
00:50:43.415 --> 00:50:46.143
I know because you know.

946
00:50:47.451 --> 00:50:52.054
There's always, always more
than what's on the surface.

947
00:50:52.126 --> 00:50:54.580
So what, the past didn't happen,
and you're not who I think you are?

948
00:50:54.604 --> 00:50:55.779
Now, don't tell me in 30 years

949
00:50:55.802 --> 00:50:58.644
that's the first time you've
asked yourself that question.

950
00:50:59.319 --> 00:51:02.638
I am not my father.
You're not getting in my head, okay?

951
00:51:02.716 --> 00:51:05.239
You think you're luring me into a trap?
You're not.

952
00:51:05.313 --> 00:51:07.916
Now, I am desperate,
which means I'm dangerous.

953
00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:11.469
My only concern is the health
and well-being of the Horsemen.

954
00:51:11.547 --> 00:51:15.025
And you're gonna lead me to them.
And if you don't, I promise you,

955
00:51:15.103 --> 00:51:18.990
you're gonna end up far worse
than in some cushy jail cell somewhere.

956
00:51:32.366 --> 00:51:35.969
What if the Horsemen
don't actually need you to save them?

957
00:51:40.518 --> 00:51:43.325
All right. So the lab
is made up of two distinct layers,

958
00:51:43.395 --> 00:51:46.441
the security perimeter
and the clean room.

959
00:51:46.512 --> 00:51:48.512
The stick we're looking for
should be in this core.

960
00:51:48.590 --> 00:51:50.636
So we're gonna have to
get underneath, extract it,

961
00:51:50.708 --> 00:51:52.435
and replace it with a decoy.

962
00:51:52.506 --> 00:51:53.920
Now, we're about to get to the chip.

963
00:51:53.944 --> 00:51:55.559
This is the thing
about the chip, it's very thin.

964
00:51:55.583 --> 00:51:58.470
Luckily, it's similar
in weight and size to a playing card,

965
00:51:58.540 --> 00:52:00.498
so we're gonna have to
brush up on our card-istry.

966
00:52:02.496 --> 00:52:03.542
Whoa, whoa!

967
00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:09.130
That's no good.

968
00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:20.844
So how do we get in?
Hannes Pike.

969
00:52:20.917 --> 00:52:22.725
He's the son of
a South African gangster.

970
00:52:22.796 --> 00:52:24.478
<i>He's RSVP'd with what looks like</i>

971
00:52:24.554 --> 00:52:26.520
<i>a couple of members
of his security detail.</i>

972
00:52:26.592 --> 00:52:29.593
<i>A doctor, to vet it, I guess,
and somebody named Buffy.</i>

973
00:52:29.669 --> 00:52:33.351
<i>Yeah, apparently, he travels
with a different bimbo every week. Hmm.</i>

974
00:52:33.425 --> 00:52:36.073
I wonder which one of us
gets to play the bimbo.

975
00:52:44.654 --> 00:52:46.495
Hey, can I get in?

976
00:52:48.210 --> 00:52:49.779
At the soothing sound of the slots

977
00:52:49.848 --> 00:52:53.213
you slip into a serene state
of slumber and sleep.

978
00:52:54.724 --> 00:52:56.247
<i>Jack, for a moment,</i>

979
00:52:56.362 --> 00:52:59.044
<i>They will think you're Hannes,
so lead them out and keep them busy.</i>

980
00:52:59.119 --> 00:53:01.324
You trust your people implicitly.

981
00:53:01.397 --> 00:53:03.443
You will have them
take care of everything,

982
00:53:03.515 --> 00:53:04.924
and you will wait in the car.

983
00:53:04.993 --> 00:53:08.551
And eyes open, wide awake. Go.

984
00:53:08.630 --> 00:53:10.484
Excuse me.
These people are following me.

985
00:53:10.508 --> 00:53:11.906
You might wanna
check their pockets.

986
00:53:15.822 --> 00:53:18.141
<i>Then our time is
ticking to infiltrate the lab</i>

987
00:53:18.220 --> 00:53:20.354
<i>and get the card to the market,
where I'll hand it off to the Eye,</i>

988
00:53:20.378 --> 00:53:21.901
<i>so we can all get the hell home.</i>

989
00:53:40.917 --> 00:53:44.202
Hannes, my old friend.
So glad you could make it.

990
00:53:44.274 --> 00:53:47.354
I knew you had a soft spot
for an old-school bidding war.

991
00:53:47.431 --> 00:53:48.511
How have you been?

992
00:53:48.590 --> 00:53:52.227
I trust my people implicitly.
What they say goes.

993
00:53:52.306 --> 00:53:55.386
Certainly. But I'm sure
you'd like to see it for yourself.

994
00:53:55.463 --> 00:53:58.270
Hold it in your hands,
check the build quality.

995
00:54:00.658 --> 00:54:03.261
Or not. Also fine.

996
00:54:03.335 --> 00:54:05.335
Well, if you'd like to follow me.

997
00:54:08.849 --> 00:54:13.248
You'll have to remove all your metal.
Watches, rings, etc.

998
00:54:13.325 --> 00:54:15.690
- Yeah, of course.
- Excuse me for asking...

999
00:54:15.763 --> 00:54:17.604
Where might I have seen you before?

1000
00:54:18.919 --> 00:54:21.363
I don't know, probably
one of my many symposiums.

1001
00:54:21.437 --> 00:54:22.517
The book jacket!

1002
00:54:22.596 --> 00:54:25.437
I have to say, Dr. Michaelakis,
I'm a huge fan.

1003
00:54:25.513 --> 00:54:27.354
Oh, thank you so much.

1004
00:54:27.431 --> 00:54:29.908
Uh, which book?
There have been so many...

1005
00:54:31.067 --> 00:54:33.796
Does Hannes' chippy
always talk for you, Doctor?

1006
00:54:35.623 --> 00:54:36.623
Doctor?

1007
00:54:39.379 --> 00:54:43.096
Sorry, when you say "chippy,"
you are referring to Buffy here, yeah?

1008
00:54:43.175 --> 00:54:44.335
Of course.

1009
00:54:46.212 --> 00:54:50.657
Hannes prefers the term "floozy"
for all his boy toys.

1010
00:54:50.728 --> 00:54:53.774
- Yeah. He does. That's good.
- Noted.

1011
00:54:53.844 --> 00:54:55.890
By the way,
I got your email, Dr. Michaelakis.

1012
00:54:55.962 --> 00:54:57.486
You had a query about our ability

1013
00:54:57.561 --> 00:54:59.641
to maintain quantum coherence
at room temperature

1014
00:54:59.719 --> 00:55:04.276
without the use of dilution,
refrigerators, or error correction.

1015
00:55:04.354 --> 00:55:05.956
Do you care to elucidate?

1016
00:55:06.032 --> 00:55:07.396
- Right.
- Yeah.

1017
00:55:07.471 --> 00:55:09.437
Yes, I did send that email.

1018
00:55:09.509 --> 00:55:12.476
- The other day, I saw you.
- I did, I sent it. So what gives?

1019
00:55:12.546 --> 00:55:13.989
I'm sorry, what gives...

1020
00:55:14.064 --> 00:55:15.588
I think what she's trying to say...

1021
00:55:15.663 --> 00:55:17.677
Actually, the grown-ups
are having a meeting, Buffy.

1022
00:55:17.701 --> 00:55:18.985
Okay.

1023
00:55:19.059 --> 00:55:20.298
If you'll hold on to that.

1024
00:55:20.378 --> 00:55:22.105
- Yeah, sure, Doctor.
- Thank you.

1025
00:55:22.176 --> 00:55:24.654
Now, every seventh grader,
even the dumb ones,

1026
00:55:24.734 --> 00:55:26.780
know the second law
of thermodynamics.

1027
00:55:26.851 --> 00:55:30.569
"All ordered systems
tend toward disorder." Disorder!

1028
00:55:30.648 --> 00:55:35.251
So I'm going to ask you one
more time, sir, what gives?

1029
00:55:40.917 --> 00:55:44.077
Well, it's actually
topological quantum order.

1030
00:55:44.194 --> 00:55:47.240
The emergence of quantum effects
on a macroscopic level.

1031
00:55:47.311 --> 00:55:50.198
So that is, in fact, what gives.

1032
00:55:55.223 --> 00:55:56.303
Thank you.

1033
00:56:02.855 --> 00:56:06.901
You know,
Buffy is not a bad name for you.

1034
00:56:06.971 --> 00:56:10.575
You know, buff, firm, taut.

1035
00:56:10.648 --> 00:56:12.502
That's good.
Did you just find a thesaurus somewhere?

1036
00:56:12.526 --> 00:56:13.810
Nubile.

1037
00:56:13.884 --> 00:56:17.010
- That's good. Thank you. Okay.
- There we go.

1038
00:56:27.231 --> 00:56:28.993
Sorry, sorry.

1039
00:56:36.062 --> 00:56:38.301
The stick's metal.
How are we going to get it out?

1040
00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:39.585
Yeah. I don't know.

1041
00:56:40.817 --> 00:56:42.580
This is the housing.

1042
00:56:42.656 --> 00:56:47.213
820 million qubit miles
of digi-synaptic resonance.

1043
00:56:52.606 --> 00:56:55.059
But of course, what sets us apart
from anything else on the planet

1044
00:56:55.083 --> 00:56:59.800
is the processor, which we playfully
refer to as the Magic Broomstick.

1045
00:56:59.878 --> 00:57:00.878
The Broomstick?

1046
00:57:00.957 --> 00:57:04.675
Has the power to clear the contents
of any computing system on the planet

1047
00:57:04.754 --> 00:57:07.402
and then magically
sweep them into your bin.

1048
00:57:07.471 --> 00:57:10.119
Oh! Broomstick. That's funny.

1049
00:57:10.188 --> 00:57:12.950
Take a look, vet it however you'd like,

1050
00:57:13.025 --> 00:57:15.344
and tell Hannes we'll be
taking bids on Monday.

1051
00:57:15.423 --> 00:57:16.628
Thank you.

1052
00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:21.382
Oh, my God.
I must be allergic to something in here.

1053
00:57:21.457 --> 00:57:23.378
What could you possibly be
allergic to in here?

1054
00:57:23.455 --> 00:57:26.661
- This room is hermetically sealed.
- That's it.

1055
00:57:26.732 --> 00:57:28.573
I'm allergic to hermetic seals.

1056
00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:30.384
If we could get him away
from the computer, please.

1057
00:57:30.408 --> 00:57:33.090
- That's the one thing.
- Just until he's recovered.

1058
00:57:33.165 --> 00:57:34.734
We're gonna need some anti-bac...

1059
00:57:35.882 --> 00:57:37.645
Do we have alcohol wipes?

1060
00:57:37.721 --> 00:57:40.094
Your hands need to be sterile
if you intend to get any closer.

1061
00:57:40.118 --> 00:57:42.323
Do I make myself clear?

1062
00:57:43.994 --> 00:57:46.801
- Sorry. We couldn't...
- No, I'm fine.

1063
00:57:46.871 --> 00:57:48.166
...take his medicine into the country.

1064
00:57:48.190 --> 00:57:49.350
Yeah...

1065
00:57:52.746 --> 00:57:55.189
Can't risk any contamination.

1066
00:57:55.263 --> 00:57:58.025
You're gonna have to stay away
from the inner workings.

1067
00:57:58.100 --> 00:57:59.464
Thank you very much.
Thank you.

1068
00:57:59.539 --> 00:58:01.193
- All right. If you're done...
- Oh. Here you go.

1069
00:58:01.217 --> 00:58:02.944
Sorry about this.
Okay. I'm okay.

1070
00:58:03.015 --> 00:58:05.141
This happens to him sometimes.
It'll pass.

1071
00:58:05.213 --> 00:58:07.100
Allergies. Nothing you can do.

1072
00:58:07.171 --> 00:58:09.529
Well, something you could do, Doctor.

1073
00:58:29.229 --> 00:58:30.229
Okay?

1074
00:58:37.261 --> 00:58:38.261
Search him, please.

1075
00:58:38.340 --> 00:58:39.784
Didn't we just do that?

1076
00:58:39.858 --> 00:58:41.188
Are you serious?

1077
00:58:41.257 --> 00:58:43.223
Okay. I wanna run
full diagnostics on the system.

1078
00:58:45.133 --> 00:58:47.020
This is completely unnecessary.

1079
00:59:16.542 --> 00:59:17.747
Up, up,

1080
00:59:17.860 --> 00:59:18.985
Okay.

1081
00:59:36.921 --> 00:59:39.047
Ah!

1082
00:59:39.119 --> 00:59:41.518
Hey! I can walk on my own.

1083
00:59:49.109 --> 00:59:50.678
Whoa!

1084
00:59:52.346 --> 00:59:53.346
Sorry.

1085
01:00:11.087 --> 01:00:12.167
Hey!

1086
01:00:12.246 --> 01:00:14.133
I can do that myself.

1087
01:00:14.204 --> 01:00:16.489
We just met, man.

1088
01:00:16.562 --> 01:00:17.925
Handsy, handsy, handsy.

1089
01:00:30.148 --> 01:00:31.148
Hey!

1090
01:00:32.306 --> 01:00:34.784
My eyes are up here, man.

1091
01:00:34.903 --> 01:00:36.313
How do you like it?

1092
01:00:58.839 --> 01:00:59.919
Hey, I'm good.

1093
01:01:01.637 --> 01:01:02.637
Turn around.

1094
01:01:20.778 --> 01:01:22.016
Do you have anything?

1095
01:01:22.096 --> 01:01:23.779
I don't know.
Can't trust anybody.

1096
01:01:25.253 --> 01:01:27.492
- I'm good.
- They're clean.

1097
01:01:28.969 --> 01:01:30.890
Do your final checks
and get them out of here.

1098
01:01:30.967 --> 01:01:33.286
Oh, we're walking
this way again. Yay.

1099
01:01:38.800 --> 01:01:42.403
I don't speak the language.

1100
01:01:48.790 --> 01:01:50.199
See?

1101
01:01:50.268 --> 01:01:52.226
What did I tell you, man?
We didn't have anything.

1102
01:01:53.145 --> 01:01:55.669
Thanks, pal. Appreciate that.

1103
01:02:11.607 --> 01:02:15.324
Something about a big white dance floor,
makes me wanna boogie.

1104
01:02:15.443 --> 01:02:17.682
Hey, thanks for having us.

1105
01:02:18.240 --> 01:02:20.161
That's great stuff.

1106
01:02:26.272 --> 01:02:27.511
Okay.

1107
01:02:27.591 --> 01:02:30.068
You guys are pretty serious
about your security, huh?

1108
01:02:33.505 --> 01:02:35.107
Okay.

1109
01:02:35.183 --> 01:02:38.627
Do I have all my things?
I feel like I came in with more stuff.

1110
01:02:38.700 --> 01:02:41.348
Did I come in with more stuff?

1111
01:02:41.417 --> 01:02:43.577
You don't think so?

1112
01:02:45.773 --> 01:02:48.296
Uh, sir, I think you
still have my wallet.

1113
01:02:48.370 --> 01:02:51.018
Remember, I, uh, gave it
to you when I came in?

1114
01:02:51.087 --> 01:02:53.247
It would be nice to leave with it.

1115
01:02:58.080 --> 01:02:59.080
Wallet coming through.

1116
01:03:06.951 --> 01:03:08.554
Sorry, my belt.

1117
01:03:14.264 --> 01:03:15.788
Uh...

1118
01:03:15.902 --> 01:03:17.868
I guess that's everything, right?

1119
01:03:17.940 --> 01:03:19.020
Thank you.

1120
01:03:19.099 --> 01:03:21.623
- That is not a toy, Buffy.
- Sorry.

1121
01:03:26.851 --> 01:03:28.420
Go, go, go!

1122
01:03:34.963 --> 01:03:36.804
All right.
Drop me off at the market

1123
01:03:36.881 --> 01:03:38.644
so I can get this to the Eye.

1124
01:03:38.720 --> 01:03:39.974
You asked me to call you
when they started

1125
01:03:39.998 --> 01:03:42.078
running around like headless chickens.

1126
01:03:43.115 --> 01:03:44.525
Well, that's what's happening.

1127
01:03:59.299 --> 01:04:01.947
This is where your sources
told you they'd be?

1128
01:04:02.016 --> 01:04:05.812
No, this is where my sources said
we might find an answer.

1129
01:04:16.961 --> 01:04:18.724
She must be familiar with your work.

1130
01:04:18.800 --> 01:04:20.209
Excuse me.

1131
01:04:20.318 --> 01:04:23.478
Miss, have you seen these people?

1132
01:04:34.184 --> 01:04:36.549
She says her grandson
spoke to their leader.

1133
01:04:36.622 --> 01:04:37.826
He's in the back.

1134
01:04:38.460 --> 01:04:39.665
What?

1135
01:04:39.779 --> 01:04:41.620
No "Wow, you speak Mandarin"?

1136
01:05:13.265 --> 01:05:15.425
Ah! The Coffin to the Beyond.

1137
01:05:16.901 --> 01:05:21.425
It was always a flap,
or a trap or mirrors.

1138
01:05:22.656 --> 01:05:26.054
The audience thought
he'd just disappeared.

1139
01:05:27.091 --> 01:05:30.012
But he was always
hidden somewhere inside.

1140
01:05:41.677 --> 01:05:46.394
I believe that's the prototype
for the one your father had made.

1141
01:05:46.472 --> 01:05:49.791
He, like many others over the years,
ordered his supplies from here.

1142
01:05:50.588 --> 01:05:53.714
Ah!
Of course.

1143
01:05:53.785 --> 01:05:57.502
That's why you gave me the look
when I said they were in Macau.

1144
01:05:57.581 --> 01:05:59.786
Your father used to come here
when you were young.

1145
01:05:59.858 --> 01:06:01.586
Can I please get some space here?

1146
01:06:06.811 --> 01:06:10.857
If you think you're the only one who'd
wondered for 30 years what happened...

1147
01:06:11.287 --> 01:06:12.935
...you're not.

1148
01:06:13.005 --> 01:06:15.324
Lionel was a master magician.

1149
01:06:15.443 --> 01:06:16.931
Either he gamed the hinges,

1150
01:06:17.001 --> 01:06:20.525
so he could kick the door
and they would pop out,

1151
01:06:20.598 --> 01:06:24.440
or he had access to the lock
from the inside so he could pick it.

1152
01:06:24.514 --> 01:06:29.197
Word is the safe was made
from cheap metal, and it bowed.

1153
01:06:29.269 --> 01:06:34.906
Still, he always had
something up his sleeve.

1154
01:06:34.983 --> 01:06:41.587
Unless he'd reached the limit of
his capabilities and his clock ran out.

1155
01:06:46.372 --> 01:06:50.895
Excuse me. My grandmother
has something you might want.

1156
01:06:54.644 --> 01:06:56.882
This was your father's.

1157
01:06:56.961 --> 01:06:59.166
He had several of these made.

1158
01:07:18.540 --> 01:07:20.188
And the Horsemen?

1159
01:07:21.097 --> 01:07:23.223
I overheard them talking.

1160
01:07:23.295 --> 01:07:27.774
One of them, Atlas, he's meeting
someone at the market nearby.

1161
01:07:44.993 --> 01:07:46.596
As for former Agent Rhodes,

1162
01:07:46.672 --> 01:07:49.275
his location is now
your number one priority.

1163
01:07:49.349 --> 01:07:51.509
We're putting out an international APB.

1164
01:07:51.587 --> 01:07:54.349
We have Interpol, including cooperation
from local authorities.

1165
01:07:54.424 --> 01:07:55.663
Agent Cowan.

1166
01:07:55.743 --> 01:07:59.346
I'm assuming it was actually not you
who walked into Newburgh Federal Prison,

1167
01:07:59.419 --> 01:08:02.340
then walked out 30 minutes later
with Thaddeus Bradley?

1168
01:08:02.416 --> 01:08:04.337
- What?
- He used your ID.

1169
01:08:08.410 --> 01:08:10.376
What are you doing, Rhodes?

1170
01:08:37.820 --> 01:08:39.866
- Atlas, hey.
- Dylan.

1171
01:08:39.938 --> 01:08:41.513
- I blew it, I'm sorry.
- What are you doing here?

1172
01:08:41.537 --> 01:08:42.776
I'm here to help now.

1173
01:08:42.855 --> 01:08:44.190
Okay, we definitely
don't need your help.

1174
01:08:44.214 --> 01:08:45.499
I know I messed up, all right?

1175
01:08:45.573 --> 01:08:47.147
Yeah, you put everybody's
lives in danger.

1176
01:08:47.171 --> 01:08:49.536
- Where is everybody?
- Where is everybody?

1177
01:08:49.609 --> 01:08:51.303
I can't be seen with you,
actually, right now.

1178
01:08:51.327 --> 01:08:53.293
I got it now, okay?
I appreciate you taking

1179
01:08:53.365 --> 01:08:54.979
the time to take control
for a little while.

1180
01:08:55.003 --> 01:08:56.738
- Hey, I'm actually... Yeah?
- Yeah, but I got it now, all right?

1181
01:08:56.762 --> 01:08:57.807
- You do?
- Yeah.

1182
01:08:57.880 --> 01:08:59.295
You look like you can
get some rest, buddy.

1183
01:08:59.319 --> 01:09:01.797
- Who you meeting here?
- I'm not meeting anybody.

1184
01:09:01.876 --> 01:09:03.411
They told me at long's
you were meeting somebody.

1185
01:09:03.435 --> 01:09:05.356
At long's?

1186
01:09:05.433 --> 01:09:08.240
Okay, yeah, no, I am, um...

1187
01:09:08.310 --> 01:09:10.709
I'm meeting somebody from the Eye.

1188
01:09:10.788 --> 01:09:12.066
Yeah, no, they agreed to meet me

1189
01:09:12.106 --> 01:09:15.107
to exchange this computer chip
for our lives back.

1190
01:09:15.183 --> 01:09:16.462
So that's what it's come to now.

1191
01:09:16.502 --> 01:09:18.356
I hope you're happy
with the situation you've put us in.

1192
01:09:18.380 --> 01:09:19.903
You're talking to the Eye?

1193
01:09:19.978 --> 01:09:21.058
Yeah.

1194
01:09:21.137 --> 01:09:22.978
Why are you talking to the Eye?

1195
01:09:23.974 --> 01:09:26.941
Atlas.
Atlas, give me the stick.

1196
01:09:27.011 --> 01:09:29.296
Dylan, you're not our
leader anymore, okay?

1197
01:09:29.369 --> 01:09:32.017
You're not our hero.
You're not FBI.

1198
01:09:32.086 --> 01:09:35.053
You're not a magician.
You're not really anything.

1199
01:09:35.123 --> 01:09:37.964
And we trusted you for a year.

1200
01:09:38.040 --> 01:09:40.494
So if you really wanna help us...
If you really...

1201
01:09:40.518 --> 01:09:43.121
If you really wanna help us,
you would leave.

1202
01:09:51.547 --> 01:09:54.831
I get it. Cool. Bye.

1203
01:10:12.486 --> 01:10:13.691
Hello, hello!

1204
01:10:13.765 --> 01:10:16.572
Yeah, that was me you called
at the magic shop.

1205
01:10:17.041 --> 01:10:20.008
So much fun though, knowing you'd be
scurrying away from me at the lab

1206
01:10:20.078 --> 01:10:21.522
just to bring it to me here.

1207
01:10:21.597 --> 01:10:23.245
Did you actually think I'd let you go?

1208
01:10:23.315 --> 01:10:25.997
- How did you do that?
- Everyone has their button.

1209
01:10:26.072 --> 01:10:30.073
You push it and they go blind.
And yours, obviously, is ego.

1210
01:10:30.148 --> 01:10:31.637
"Stay the course.

1211
01:10:31.707 --> 01:10:34.469
<i>Great changes are in store.</i>

1212
01:10:34.544 --> 01:10:37.067
<i>Trust that your unique talents</i>

1213
01:10:37.141 --> 01:10:38.710
will not go unrecognized."

1214
01:10:39.978 --> 01:10:41.422
- That was you.
- Yeah.

1215
01:10:41.497 --> 01:10:43.020
Merritt downloaded all the files,

1216
01:10:43.095 --> 01:10:45.823
but how do you think
we were able to find him?

1217
01:10:45.892 --> 01:10:46.972
Your phone.

1218
01:10:48.889 --> 01:10:50.855
I asked to see the face
behind all of this.

1219
01:10:50.927 --> 01:10:54.087
What you actually put your phone on
was a bi-wave siphon pad.

1220
01:10:55.603 --> 01:10:59.808
Once again,
science beats magic.

1221
01:11:00.278 --> 01:11:02.563
- So, the stick?
- No.

1222
01:11:05.673 --> 01:11:06.673
I got it!

1223
01:11:06.752 --> 01:11:08.195
I got it. I got it.

1224
01:11:10.228 --> 01:11:12.115
- Fuck, Dylan!
- Wait, leave them!

1225
01:11:12.186 --> 01:11:13.681
You really think
I'm gonna walk away from you?

1226
01:11:13.705 --> 01:11:15.785
Give me the stick!

1227
01:11:15.902 --> 01:11:16.982
Give me it!

1228
01:11:17.061 --> 01:11:20.028
All right.
Don't ever question me again. Beat it!

1229
01:11:30.847 --> 01:11:33.325
- So, the stick.
- Go to hell.

1230
01:11:34.164 --> 01:11:35.164
Boys.

1231
01:11:47.631 --> 01:11:49.552
I'm sorry! I'm sorry!

1232
01:12:05.852 --> 01:12:07.262
There's like 10 of you.

1233
01:12:07.331 --> 01:12:09.138
Come on, he's just doing tricks.

1234
01:12:09.209 --> 01:12:11.096
He's there! Go!

1235
01:12:59.719 --> 01:13:00.799
Oh...

1236
01:13:34.564 --> 01:13:39.360
Dylan, watch the pretty birdies fly.
And sleep.

1237
01:13:39.719 --> 01:13:43.003
- Doesn't work on me.
- Not even as a distraction?

1238
01:13:45.713 --> 01:13:48.316
- Good job.
- Nice to be involved.

1239
01:13:56.582 --> 01:13:58.628
Hey, guys! Guys! Hey!

1240
01:13:58.700 --> 01:14:01.393
- I just saw Dylan, something happened.
- What do you mean you just saw Dylan?

1241
01:14:01.417 --> 01:14:03.463
Dylan's here?
What happened?

1242
01:14:03.535 --> 01:14:06.012
- Atlas.
- I don't know.

1243
01:14:10.847 --> 01:14:12.007
What are they doing?

1244
01:14:12.086 --> 01:14:14.530
Those are Walter's men.

1245
01:14:14.604 --> 01:14:17.650
This is what they want.

1246
01:14:17.721 --> 01:14:19.925
What the hell?
How do you still have that?

1247
01:14:24.873 --> 01:14:26.681
Whoa. That's Lionel Shrike's safe.

1248
01:14:26.752 --> 01:14:29.275
What the hell
do they want with that?

1249
01:14:29.349 --> 01:14:31.111
Okay, we gotta
follow that truck.

1250
01:14:31.187 --> 01:14:34.506
They must have Dylan.
We don't have much time.

1251
01:14:38.899 --> 01:14:40.138
What do you want?

1252
01:14:40.218 --> 01:14:44.855
You to understand something
very important.

1253
01:14:44.933 --> 01:14:48.173
We are mirror images.

1254
01:14:48.250 --> 01:14:49.580
You see, because like you,

1255
01:14:49.649 --> 01:14:51.887
I share a very deep connection
with my father.

1256
01:14:53.245 --> 01:14:58.201
And like you, I'll stop at nothing
to avenge any wrongs done to him.

1257
01:15:02.875 --> 01:15:06.433
You know the only difference
between your father and mine?

1258
01:15:06.512 --> 01:15:08.399
Mine's still alive.

1259
01:15:09.109 --> 01:15:10.314
Get him up.

1260
01:15:23.814 --> 01:15:28.134
So, this is the man

1261
01:15:28.210 --> 01:15:31.290
who spent a lifetime
plotting against me.

1262
01:15:33.285 --> 01:15:35.604
I wouldn't have known your face

1263
01:15:35.683 --> 01:15:39.161
if it had been on a billboard
outside my bedroom window.

1264
01:15:39.239 --> 01:15:42.921
But you have spent, what,
30 years thinking about mine?

1265
01:15:42.995 --> 01:15:44.280
When my father died,

1266
01:15:44.354 --> 01:15:46.480
your insurance company
denied my mother her claims.

1267
01:15:46.552 --> 01:15:47.926
- I wouldn't even have known...
- She died a pauper!

1268
01:15:47.950 --> 01:15:49.792
...who your mother was.

1269
01:15:49.868 --> 01:15:54.472
She was a digit, a nanobyte
in a stream of data.

1270
01:15:54.544 --> 01:15:56.510
Because nothing is personal to you

1271
01:15:56.582 --> 01:15:58.915
and everyone and everything
is just a number, isn't that so?

1272
01:15:58.939 --> 01:16:01.543
That is where you are wrong,
Mr. Rhodes.

1273
01:16:01.617 --> 01:16:06.539
Once you made yourself known
it became very personal.

1274
01:16:07.810 --> 01:16:09.652
- My son...
- Yeah, the sociopath.

1275
01:16:09.729 --> 01:16:14.286
Funny thing is, I have seven
so-called legitimate children.

1276
01:16:14.364 --> 01:16:17.410
Sniveling, snobbish, stupid runts.

1277
01:16:17.481 --> 01:16:21.448
I have one illegitimate child
who is a lot like me.

1278
01:16:21.517 --> 01:16:23.881
The point is,
he wants something,

1279
01:16:23.954 --> 01:16:29.115
and like any good parent, I will
stop at nothing to get it for him.

1280
01:16:29.189 --> 01:16:30.916
Your Horsemen have it.

1281
01:16:31.027 --> 01:16:35.312
You will turn it and them
over to us, now.

1282
01:16:35.383 --> 01:16:36.463
No.

1283
01:16:40.218 --> 01:16:42.980
Dylan, I would like you
to really take this moment in

1284
01:16:43.055 --> 01:16:46.454
because this is the consequence
of your personal vendetta come to life

1285
01:16:46.532 --> 01:16:48.294
and staring you in the face.

1286
01:16:48.370 --> 01:16:50.132
I want you to know
that when you're gone,

1287
01:16:50.208 --> 01:16:51.254
we're not going to stop.

1288
01:16:51.327 --> 01:16:53.453
We're gonna destroy
everything you believe in,

1289
01:16:53.525 --> 01:16:55.730
starting with the Horsemen,
and ending with the Eye.

1290
01:16:55.802 --> 01:16:57.690
Now then, cast your mind back

1291
01:16:57.761 --> 01:17:00.886
to standing by the banks of the river
watching your father descend,

1292
01:17:00.957 --> 01:17:02.957
disgraced and discredited.

1293
01:17:03.035 --> 01:17:05.798
The horrid psychic pain
as you have relived for years.

1294
01:17:06.552 --> 01:17:09.836
I felt that same thing two years ago

1295
01:17:09.908 --> 01:17:13.034
as you drained not just my father's
fortune, but his dignity!

1296
01:17:13.585 --> 01:17:15.585
And yet you took pleasure from it.

1297
01:17:18.420 --> 01:17:21.023
This should bring back
some childhood memories.

1298
01:17:48.390 --> 01:17:50.231
- Would you like some tea?
- Oh. Thank you.

1299
01:18:05.932 --> 01:18:08.217
- You ready?
- Over to you.

1300
01:18:15.203 --> 01:18:16.249
Splash!

1301
01:18:19.799 --> 01:18:21.162
Job done!

1302
01:18:36.782 --> 01:18:40.180
Hell is going to
seem like a day at the spa

1303
01:18:40.258 --> 01:18:42.736
by the time we're through
with these Horsemen.

1304
01:18:42.815 --> 01:18:45.464
- Wouldn't you say?
- You had me at "hell."

1305
01:19:02.356 --> 01:19:04.721
<i>I love you so much.</i>

1306
01:19:07.311 --> 01:19:09.710
Always keep something up my sleeve.

1307
01:20:28.430 --> 01:20:31.476
I wish I could dredge
from the muck of my soul

1308
01:20:31.547 --> 01:20:35.628
one speck of remorse,
but I can't.

1309
01:20:35.703 --> 01:20:42.147
So I'll stick to the business at hand
and try not to smile too broadly.

1310
01:20:42.216 --> 01:20:47.536
And, uh, thank you for delivering
Mr. Dylan Rhodes.

1311
01:20:48.330 --> 01:20:50.057
I take it it's done.

1312
01:20:51.367 --> 01:20:53.333
Literally hit the bottom.

1313
01:20:53.924 --> 01:20:59.688
- Now, where are the Horsemen?
- They're performers.

1314
01:20:59.759 --> 01:21:05.317
Their pride, their ego, their need
to be adored are all too powerful

1315
01:21:05.393 --> 01:21:07.280
to keep them out of
the limelight for long.

1316
01:21:07.351 --> 01:21:12.829
If they don't make themselves known,
you will find yourself

1317
01:21:12.945 --> 01:21:16.912
in a very similar position
to Mr. Rhodes.

1318
01:21:16.981 --> 01:21:20.823
- Are we clear about that?
- As clear as the water he's under.

1319
01:21:28.290 --> 01:21:31.847
You'll have the rest
when I have the Horsemen.

1320
01:22:04.094 --> 01:22:05.140
Dylan!

1321
01:22:06.612 --> 01:22:08.930
Dylan, come on, buddy, breathe!

1322
01:22:09.009 --> 01:22:10.214
- Dylan.
- Dylan?

1323
01:22:10.288 --> 01:22:12.368
Dylan, come on!

1324
01:22:16.642 --> 01:22:19.370
You're okay.

1325
01:22:19.439 --> 01:22:22.121
Jesus! You scared
the hell out of us, man.

1326
01:22:22.236 --> 01:22:25.839
- Are you all right, bro?
- Okay!

1327
01:22:25.912 --> 01:22:27.878
- Thank you!
- This is the least I could do.

1328
01:22:27.950 --> 01:22:29.924
No. There's a hell of a lot
less you could've done.

1329
01:22:29.948 --> 01:22:31.278
That was not an option.

1330
01:22:31.347 --> 01:22:33.234
You took a bullet for us, it was huge.

1331
01:22:33.305 --> 01:22:35.146
You really did, man.

1332
01:22:37.940 --> 01:22:39.623
Thanks for pulling me out.
Seriously.

1333
01:22:40.578 --> 01:22:41.941
Yeah.

1334
01:22:42.016 --> 01:22:44.142
You got yourself out.
We did the easy part.

1335
01:22:44.214 --> 01:22:46.453
Literally, in my case,
I was just standing on the shore

1336
01:22:46.532 --> 01:22:47.906
while Atlas jumped down and got you.

1337
01:22:47.930 --> 01:22:49.454
I'm serious.

1338
01:22:50.048 --> 01:22:51.935
Hey, you guys.
Something's not right.

1339
01:22:52.006 --> 01:22:53.734
- What's wrong?
- What?

1340
01:22:55.323 --> 01:22:57.642
- It's fake.
- What?

1341
01:22:59.399 --> 01:23:00.922
No, I had the stick.

1342
01:23:00.997 --> 01:23:03.646
The actual stick.
I swear, it was in my hand,

1343
01:23:03.715 --> 01:23:06.238
in the lab, and then I put it in
the little case thing.

1344
01:23:06.312 --> 01:23:08.486
- And I gave it to Atlas.
- Right, which I then gave to Dylan.

1345
01:23:08.510 --> 01:23:11.033
Is there any chance you've possibly
misplaced it at any point?

1346
01:23:11.107 --> 01:23:14.472
No, I took it from you
and then slipped it back in your coat

1347
01:23:14.544 --> 01:23:16.306
when I pushed you out.

1348
01:23:16.382 --> 01:23:19.588
Either way, we're left holding
a whole sack full of nada.

1349
01:23:19.659 --> 01:23:22.262
Yeah, but they're gonna come after us
as if we do have something.

1350
01:23:22.336 --> 01:23:25.701
No. Okay. I agree that somebody
got the better of us. Okay? Somehow.

1351
01:23:25.773 --> 01:23:28.171
But I don't agree
that we have a sack full of nada.

1352
01:23:28.250 --> 01:23:31.410
Because we're all here.
That's a sack full of something.

1353
01:23:32.406 --> 01:23:39.089
Listen, we all know that Walter is not
going to stop until he gets that chip.

1354
01:23:39.159 --> 01:23:42.717
And we're the only ones
that even know that he's alive.

1355
01:23:42.796 --> 01:23:47.399
We can't just walk away.
That's not what the Horsemen do.

1356
01:23:50.188 --> 01:23:51.995
There's a quote. Um...

1357
01:23:52.066 --> 01:23:53.476
It's actually by the magician

1358
01:23:53.545 --> 01:23:55.343
that made me wanna do this
in the first place.

1359
01:23:55.383 --> 01:23:57.304
It's, uh...

1360
01:23:57.381 --> 01:24:04.064
"A magician's greatest power lies
forever shrouded in his empty fist."

1361
01:24:04.134 --> 01:24:06.453
"And the very idea
that he can convince the world

1362
01:24:06.532 --> 01:24:09.692
that he is, in fact,
carrying with him a secret."

1363
01:24:09.769 --> 01:24:12.610
- That's Lionel Shrike.
- Right.

1364
01:24:13.844 --> 01:24:14.979
So what are you saying?

1365
01:24:15.003 --> 01:24:18.209
That we should pretend like
we have something we don't?

1366
01:24:21.277 --> 01:24:24.676
- What is Walter's greatest fear?
- Being seen.

1367
01:24:24.754 --> 01:24:28.755
- And what does the stick do?
- The stick can unencode anything.

1368
01:24:28.829 --> 01:24:33.752
It can open up a back door
into any computer on the planet.

1369
01:24:33.824 --> 01:24:35.824
- Including his.
- Yeah.

1370
01:24:37.461 --> 01:24:39.904
Right, we not only make him
think that we have it,

1371
01:24:39.978 --> 01:24:44.059
but that we're going to use it
to expose and destroy him.

1372
01:24:44.134 --> 01:24:47.499
We need a plan.
But we don't have time.

1373
01:24:47.611 --> 01:24:51.054
We have each other
and there are five of us.

1374
01:24:51.127 --> 01:24:54.048
But we also need supplies, right?
And resources.

1375
01:24:54.124 --> 01:24:57.409
And probably more than five people.

1376
01:24:57.481 --> 01:25:01.368
Do you really think
you don't have help?

1377
01:25:01.437 --> 01:25:04.199
Oh, she speaks English.
Of course. Yeah.

1378
01:25:04.274 --> 01:25:09.673
Do you know why the Fool is
the most powerful card in the tarot?

1379
01:25:09.749 --> 01:25:13.386
Not because the person
who draws him is a fool.

1380
01:25:13.465 --> 01:25:17.909
He's a fool because he's a clean slate,

1381
01:25:17.980 --> 01:25:21.901
and, therefore, can become anything.

1382
01:25:21.976 --> 01:25:23.261
You were chosen.

1383
01:25:24.054 --> 01:25:25.861
All of you.

1384
01:25:25.932 --> 01:25:31.138
Not because of who you are,
but because of who you might become.

1385
01:25:32.766 --> 01:25:36.608
When you say "chosen"...
Are you part of the Eye?

1386
01:25:36.682 --> 01:25:40.728
Yes. We are part of the Eye.

1387
01:25:40.798 --> 01:25:44.003
You've each doubted our existence.

1388
01:25:44.833 --> 01:25:49.153
Wondering if we,
indeed, were watching.

1389
01:25:52.746 --> 01:25:56.383
We are.
We always have been.

1390
01:25:56.462 --> 01:25:58.985
This is the best
magic shop in the world.

1391
01:25:59.059 --> 01:26:01.264
- You know what she's saying, right?
- Yes.

1392
01:26:01.337 --> 01:26:03.736
That now we have
resources and help.

1393
01:26:05.533 --> 01:26:06.896
Thank you.

1394
01:26:09.369 --> 01:26:11.051
So, who's in?

1395
01:26:14.843 --> 01:26:17.447
- I'm in.
- Yeah, I'm in.

1396
01:26:20.278 --> 01:26:21.278
Let's do this.

1397
01:26:38.979 --> 01:26:40.662
No, no, no.

1398
01:26:43.295 --> 01:26:45.421
<i>Not long ago, we were tricked.</i>

1399
01:26:45.493 --> 01:26:48.414
<i>I was exposed
and brought back from the dead.</i>

1400
01:26:48.490 --> 01:26:51.331
<i>So we thought it was only fitting
that we do the same thing</i>

1401
01:26:51.407 --> 01:26:55.772
<i>- to the person who did it to us.
- Midnight, New Year's Eve. London.</i>

1402
01:26:55.842 --> 01:26:59.082
<i>We will be performing a series of shows
that all connect to our big finale,</i>

1403
01:26:59.159 --> 01:27:01.319
<i>so pay close attention to each trick.</i>

1404
01:27:01.397 --> 01:27:02.477
Get my father.

1405
01:27:04.634 --> 01:27:05.714
Run!

1406
01:27:05.793 --> 01:27:08.952
I want Interpol, Scotland Yard,
whoever we have in London.

1407
01:27:09.069 --> 01:27:10.308
What do we know about Rhodes?

1408
01:27:10.388 --> 01:27:12.229
Wherever the Horsemen are,
that's where he is.

1409
01:27:12.306 --> 01:27:13.431
Let's get to the airport.

1410
01:27:13.505 --> 01:27:15.958
<i>So leave your kiddies
at home and get ready for a wild night,</i>

1411
01:27:15.982 --> 01:27:19.062
<i>because we, too, will bring
someone back from the dead.</i>

1412
01:27:19.139 --> 01:27:20.946
Let's move, everyone!

1413
01:27:21.017 --> 01:27:22.951
<i>In front of
the largest stage in the world...</i>

1414
01:27:22.975 --> 01:27:24.896
<i>The actual world.</i>

1415
01:27:24.973 --> 01:27:28.736
You know they're playing you, right?
Leading you into a trap?

1416
01:27:28.809 --> 01:27:30.458
Yeah, but the threat is real.

1417
01:27:30.568 --> 01:27:33.216
This card can hack into any system
on the planet, including ours.

1418
01:27:33.285 --> 01:27:35.524
They have it, why wouldn't
they use it to expose us?

1419
01:27:35.603 --> 01:27:37.091
Me. Everything we've done.

1420
01:27:37.161 --> 01:27:39.207
Is it real, Mr. McKinney?

1421
01:27:46.592 --> 01:27:47.830
The threat is real.

1422
01:27:47.910 --> 01:27:50.195
So, how do we stay
ahead of them?

1423
01:27:50.268 --> 01:27:52.633
We need to get
to London by midnight.

1424
01:28:01.337 --> 01:28:02.911
<i>We are here in London</i>

1425
01:28:02.935 --> 01:28:05.413
<i>on this New Year's Eve,
and the crowds are out in force.</i>

1426
01:28:05.493 --> 01:28:07.027
<i>Locals and tourists alike</i>

1427
01:28:07.051 --> 01:28:09.177
<i>getting ready for
the party of the year.</i>

1428
01:28:09.249 --> 01:28:11.223
<i>A video gone viral from the infamous</i>

1429
01:28:11.247 --> 01:28:13.088
<i>Horsemen magicians has surfaced.</i>

1430
01:28:13.165 --> 01:28:14.899
<i>They promise
to make a grand reappearance.</i>

1431
01:28:14.923 --> 01:28:18.003
<i>But will it end in
their arrest on national television?</i>

1432
01:28:18.080 --> 01:28:19.763
<i>Or some sort of vindication?</i>

1433
01:28:19.838 --> 01:28:21.918
<i>Who knows where they'll show up...</i>

1434
01:28:21.996 --> 01:28:23.451
<i>But we do know, wherever,</i>

1435
01:28:23.475 --> 01:28:26.601
<i>whenever that is,
people will be watching.</i>

1436
01:28:28.310 --> 01:28:30.231
Yeah, texts, tweets, Instagrams.

1437
01:28:30.308 --> 01:28:33.275
Anything that says "Horsemen,"
brags about seeing the Horsemen,

1438
01:28:33.345 --> 01:28:35.027
tag them and bundle them.

1439
01:28:35.103 --> 01:28:37.468
Dad, who do we have security-wise
on this? Your guys?

1440
01:28:37.541 --> 01:28:38.701
Whoever you want.

1441
01:28:38.819 --> 01:28:40.698
Well, we want all of them
and all their friends.

1442
01:28:40.778 --> 01:28:42.871
Anyone who wants to work
stationed throughout the city,

1443
01:28:42.895 --> 01:28:44.703
so they can be anywhere in 60 seconds.

1444
01:28:58.959 --> 01:29:01.244
Hello! Hey!

1445
01:29:03.195 --> 01:29:04.957
My name's Jack Wilder.

1446
01:29:05.793 --> 01:29:07.759
Goddamn, it feels good to be back.

1447
01:29:07.830 --> 01:29:11.434
All right, gather around. Indulge me.

1448
01:29:11.507 --> 01:29:13.712
I wanna talk to you about
something real quick.

1449
01:29:13.785 --> 01:29:17.672
You all know Three Card Monte,
also known as "Find the Lady"?

1450
01:29:17.741 --> 01:29:22.060
Trick performed by street hustlers all
over the world. And why street hustlers?

1451
01:29:22.136 --> 01:29:25.023
Because as soon as you walk up
to the table, you lose.

1452
01:29:25.093 --> 01:29:27.059
Every time.
Let me demonstrate.

1453
01:29:29.529 --> 01:29:33.655
While we're at it,
let's find ourselves a life-sized Queen.

1454
01:29:33.725 --> 01:29:35.646
Ma'am, would you
mind being my Queen?

1455
01:29:38.959 --> 01:29:41.324
Don't worry. I promise you
there's a 50 percent chance

1456
01:29:41.397 --> 01:29:42.732
you'll get her back after the show.

1457
01:29:42.756 --> 01:29:44.563
Head over there.
Thank you very much.

1458
01:29:44.634 --> 01:29:48.157
All right. Let's find ourselves
some Jacks to serve our Queen!

1459
01:29:48.230 --> 01:29:49.515
<i>Trust me, gentlemen.</i>

1460
01:29:49.589 --> 01:29:53.351
I know how these guys work now.
They're gonna start with a tease.

1461
01:29:53.425 --> 01:29:55.391
A few little pop-ups.

1462
01:29:55.463 --> 01:29:58.350
<i>Something to lure you in. Like...</i>

1463
01:29:58.420 --> 01:30:02.546
Well, you know, like Three Card Monte,
where the guy goes,

1464
01:30:02.616 --> 01:30:05.184
"Looky here, but no, no, looky here!"

1465
01:30:05.253 --> 01:30:09.174
<i>Except when all these
little tricks are done,</i>

1466
01:30:09.249 --> 01:30:11.329
<i>the only thing
you're gonna be looking at</i>

1467
01:30:11.407 --> 01:30:15.408
is the inside of your own colon,
with the words

1468
01:30:15.483 --> 01:30:18.404
<i>"Horsemen" written all over it.</i>

1469
01:30:19.119 --> 01:30:20.688
In blood.

1470
01:30:20.758 --> 01:30:24.236
We get the picture, Mr. McKinney.

1471
01:30:24.314 --> 01:30:26.360
And get out of my chair.

1472
01:30:26.432 --> 01:30:28.637
Hello, everybody!

1473
01:30:28.710 --> 01:30:30.676
Come close, come on!

1474
01:30:30.748 --> 01:30:32.669
All right, all right, so listen.

1475
01:30:32.746 --> 01:30:38.224
Tonight, I am going to make
something fly using actual magic.

1476
01:30:38.300 --> 01:30:40.062
- Are you ready?
- Yeah!

1477
01:30:40.138 --> 01:30:43.184
I'm going to take
a very large vessel,

1478
01:30:43.255 --> 01:30:47.620
I'm gonna make it fly over there
and land on the Thames.

1479
01:30:47.691 --> 01:30:49.532
Can you handle that?

1480
01:30:49.609 --> 01:30:51.132
Same rules apply as before.

1481
01:30:51.207 --> 01:30:53.287
All you need to do is
keep your eye on the Queen.

1482
01:30:53.365 --> 01:30:54.729
It's as simple as that.

1483
01:30:54.803 --> 01:30:56.531
- Are you guys ready?
- Yeah!

1484
01:30:56.602 --> 01:30:58.045
Dad, look at this.

1485
01:30:59.399 --> 01:31:00.774
Tag this one.
Where does it come from?

1486
01:31:00.798 --> 01:31:02.923
Near the river.
Looks like the shows are starting.

1487
01:31:02.995 --> 01:31:04.570
But we're not the only ones
who found them.

1488
01:31:04.594 --> 01:31:06.232
- What do you mean?
- It's on Channel One.

1489
01:31:14.464 --> 01:31:15.719
- Let's go to 2.
- Here we go.

1490
01:31:15.743 --> 01:31:18.346
Got him. We have a location.

1491
01:31:18.420 --> 01:31:20.386
It's Jack Wilder.
Here come the Horsemen.

1492
01:31:20.458 --> 01:31:22.424
Okay, we're moving.
Let's go, let's go.

1493
01:31:22.496 --> 01:31:24.223
Typing in grid coordinates now.

1494
01:31:26.012 --> 01:31:28.661
Turn around.
Hide yourselves with the cards.

1495
01:31:29.289 --> 01:31:32.495
And here we go. Switch!

1496
01:31:33.964 --> 01:31:36.138
You guys still have your eye on her?
You want me to go back and do it?

1497
01:31:36.162 --> 01:31:39.242
Okay, all right. I'm moving on.
And switch.

1498
01:31:41.237 --> 01:31:42.283
Where is she?

1499
01:31:43.635 --> 01:31:45.681
Down at the end?
You sure?

1500
01:31:45.753 --> 01:31:48.560
All right, can I get a drumroll
from my man in the back?

1501
01:31:48.630 --> 01:31:50.551
Thank you very much.

1502
01:31:51.787 --> 01:31:53.310
Uh-oh.

1503
01:31:54.064 --> 01:31:55.189
Uh-oh.

1504
01:31:56.462 --> 01:31:58.939
There's my girl.

1505
01:31:59.019 --> 01:32:01.623
Not my girl, 100 percent your girl.

1506
01:32:01.737 --> 01:32:03.066
But just in this situation.

1507
01:32:03.135 --> 01:32:05.784
Okay, but I gotta warm up
a little bit first. Okay?

1508
01:32:05.852 --> 01:32:09.740
So, uh, let me just
make something small fly, like...

1509
01:32:09.808 --> 01:32:11.934
Fly, fly, fly!

1510
01:32:12.006 --> 01:32:15.405
Wow, right? Look at that!
Aw. Come on, people.

1511
01:32:15.523 --> 01:32:18.410
Tough crowd. You, fly!

1512
01:32:18.480 --> 01:32:19.605
- Me?
- Check your fly.

1513
01:32:22.476 --> 01:32:24.715
You like that?

1514
01:32:24.794 --> 01:32:26.681
You support that relationship?

1515
01:32:26.752 --> 01:32:29.639
That guy had a bird in his pants.
That's disgusting.

1516
01:32:33.305 --> 01:32:35.399
<i>Copy that.
We've got two sightings reported.</i>

1517
01:32:35.423 --> 01:32:38.344
<i>- Heading to Greenwich Market.</i>
- Get me Thaddeus.

1518
01:32:38.420 --> 01:32:40.705
<i>Thaddeus, the FBI are here.
They're moving.</i>

1519
01:32:40.817 --> 01:32:43.897
Let 'em move.
That's what the Horsemen want.

1520
01:32:43.974 --> 01:32:45.709
Well, what if they get there first?
That can't happen.

1521
01:32:45.733 --> 01:32:47.858
<i>I guarantee you, they won't.</i>

1522
01:32:47.930 --> 01:32:49.135
Find the pattern.

1523
01:32:50.088 --> 01:32:53.055
You do that, and you'll be able
to cut them off

1524
01:32:53.125 --> 01:32:55.524
<i>before they get to their final trick.</i>

1525
01:32:55.603 --> 01:32:59.320
If it makes you feel better,
I'll take care of the FBI.

1526
01:33:12.586 --> 01:33:15.029
OK, well, I guess you found me!

1527
01:33:15.103 --> 01:33:16.672
We've got a third spot.

1528
01:33:16.742 --> 01:33:19.265
Okay, everybody,
we're getting a pattern.

1529
01:33:21.617 --> 01:33:26.698
Urn... Give me two, 13 and 21.

1530
01:33:27.771 --> 01:33:30.658
What the hell is that?
Freeze that and zoom in.

1531
01:33:32.766 --> 01:33:35.528
How the hell did this happen?

1532
01:33:35.643 --> 01:33:38.769
He's dead. We watched him drown.
How the hell did this happen?

1533
01:33:38.839 --> 01:33:41.363
Given that it did happen,
the pertinent question is,

1534
01:33:41.437 --> 01:33:42.492
what are we gonna do about it?

1535
01:33:42.516 --> 01:33:44.278
We're gonna stop him, Dad.
We'll stop him.

1536
01:33:44.354 --> 01:33:45.434
Where's my coat?

1537
01:33:45.513 --> 01:33:48.241
Walter, for the record,
I never loved the safe idea.

1538
01:33:48.310 --> 01:33:49.868
Oh, I know
where they're going.

1539
01:33:49.908 --> 01:33:52.512
The next show is Merritt's
and it's by the river.

1540
01:33:52.586 --> 01:33:53.995
Chase, you need
to get to him

1541
01:33:54.064 --> 01:33:56.302
so we can find the Horsemen's
entry point for the finale.

1542
01:34:01.896 --> 01:34:05.534
I have a confession to make. I've been
told I have some control issues.

1543
01:34:05.653 --> 01:34:08.971
I've learned that it's really,
really hard to control people,

1544
01:34:09.049 --> 01:34:12.970
so I'm gonna try to control something
that's a lot easier than people.

1545
01:34:13.045 --> 01:34:14.484
I'm gonna try to
control the weather.

1546
01:34:14.524 --> 01:34:17.286
Yeah, rain, um...

1547
01:34:18.800 --> 01:34:20.494
It'd be a little difficult
to make it rain, right?

1548
01:34:20.518 --> 01:34:23.280
That would be something
that only God can do, right?

1549
01:34:23.355 --> 01:34:25.640
I'm gonna do something
that God can't do.

1550
01:34:25.713 --> 01:34:28.600
I'm not just gonna make it clear up.
No, no, no.

1551
01:34:28.670 --> 01:34:30.750
I'm gonna make it actually stop.

1552
01:34:44.054 --> 01:34:47.260
And can God do that?
Mmm. No, I don't think so.

1553
01:34:47.331 --> 01:34:49.696
Or what about make it go up?

1554
01:34:57.361 --> 01:34:59.361
Are you still with me?

1555
01:34:59.439 --> 01:35:02.485
Watch the Queen on the left.
Let's do it. Turn around.

1556
01:35:05.832 --> 01:35:06.957
Switch!

1557
01:35:10.668 --> 01:35:11.827
Show me the lady.

1558
01:35:11.906 --> 01:35:14.714
Show me the lady!

1559
01:35:14.784 --> 01:35:15.988
The end? End?

1560
01:35:18.460 --> 01:35:20.618
You know what?
I'm actually starting to feel kinda bad.

1561
01:35:21.577 --> 01:35:24.703
It's just because I can actually feel
that you guys are paying attention.

1562
01:35:24.774 --> 01:35:26.376
It's just that...

1563
01:35:26.452 --> 01:35:29.895
You know, it's unfortunate
that it's the wrong kind of attention.

1564
01:35:31.127 --> 01:35:33.605
Yeah, we need a fresher bird.

1565
01:35:34.803 --> 01:35:37.883
He's so cute. He's so cute!

1566
01:35:37.960 --> 01:35:40.723
Let's kill him, right?

1567
01:35:40.798 --> 01:35:44.765
- Merritt, move into position.
- Got it, Dylan.

1568
01:35:44.833 --> 01:35:46.516
Uh, I'm on my way.
I'll see you there.

1569
01:35:46.592 --> 01:35:48.956
Lula, stand by, all right?

1570
01:35:49.069 --> 01:35:51.388
Or, what about just go,
I don't know, just go insane?

1571
01:36:00.937 --> 01:36:03.381
<i>Reports are
coming in from all over London</i>

1572
01:36:03.455 --> 01:36:07.058
<i>as the Horsemen appear to have taken
over the whole city for their show.</i>

1573
01:36:10.088 --> 01:36:13.771
Hey, broski-whaddya-knowski?
Where you headed?

1574
01:36:13.844 --> 01:36:15.179
- Please, bro, don't do this.
- Where are you headed?

1575
01:36:15.203 --> 01:36:17.010
North, south, east?

1576
01:36:17.880 --> 01:36:19.404
East. Tower of London?

1577
01:36:19.479 --> 01:36:22.241
Tower Bridge?
Near Tower Bridge? The Thames!

1578
01:36:22.316 --> 01:36:26.839
You're going to
the Thamesy-Whemsy! You're so easy!

1579
01:36:26.911 --> 01:36:29.560
Bro, if there's any chance of us
ever having a relationship...

1580
01:36:29.629 --> 01:36:32.027
There's not.
Where's your entry?

1581
01:36:32.106 --> 01:36:35.902
South Bank? Greenwich? Greenwich.

1582
01:36:35.982 --> 01:36:39.586
I know where you're going.
I know where...

1583
01:36:40.937 --> 01:36:42.301
Watch it, hobo.

1584
01:36:45.213 --> 01:36:47.737
Hey, Merritt. Oh, come on.
Tell me you're kidding. How?

1585
01:36:49.529 --> 01:36:50.892
When?

1586
01:36:50.967 --> 01:36:53.616
Shit. All right. All right, I got it.

1587
01:36:53.685 --> 01:36:55.731
Don't worry about it.
I'll warn the others.

1588
01:36:58.680 --> 01:37:00.362
Sorry. Just...

1589
01:37:02.995 --> 01:37:04.170
What? I just told you...

1590
01:37:04.194 --> 01:37:05.194
Phone call.

1591
01:37:06.592 --> 01:37:07.672
- Hello?
<i>- Lula.</i>

1592
01:37:07.751 --> 01:37:11.115
Dylan. Ah, yeah, kind of in the middle
of something right now.

1593
01:37:11.187 --> 01:37:13.756
<i>Wrap it up.
Meet me at the rendezvous point now.</i>

1594
01:37:13.824 --> 01:37:15.109
- What?
<i>- Now!</i>

1595
01:37:15.783 --> 01:37:17.749
Was that an act of God?

1596
01:37:17.860 --> 01:37:20.940
No. No, no, no,
that was an act of me

1597
01:37:21.017 --> 01:37:24.177
And the strobe lights
and rain machines.

1598
01:37:24.254 --> 01:37:26.175
And I hope you guys
were paying attention.

1599
01:37:26.252 --> 01:37:28.610
Because you're gonna need to know
all of that for the final.

1600
01:37:32.006 --> 01:37:35.086
So how did I do it?
Did I use a Barry Trap?

1601
01:37:35.163 --> 01:37:37.482
Maybe some switches
or a few duplicates?

1602
01:37:37.561 --> 01:37:38.607
<i>Wham Jack, go now!</i>

1603
01:37:38.680 --> 01:37:39.775
You know what?
I hate to say it.

1604
01:37:39.799 --> 01:37:41.597
You're gonna have to wait to find out.
I know.

1605
01:37:41.637 --> 01:37:44.035
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.
I love you all.

1606
01:37:47.471 --> 01:37:48.551
<i>Lula, go!</i>

1607
01:37:48.630 --> 01:37:50.710
I have to go.

1608
01:38:09.569 --> 01:38:13.775
Out of the way, FBI!
Move, move, move! Go!

1609
01:38:13.844 --> 01:38:15.572
Get some men in that park!

1610
01:38:15.643 --> 01:38:16.768
Men in the park, now!

1611
01:38:16.841 --> 01:38:18.967
You gonna fill up on appetizers?

1612
01:38:19.039 --> 01:38:21.688
Or save room for
the main course?

1613
01:38:21.757 --> 01:38:23.564
Where the hell
are the Horsemen?

1614
01:38:23.635 --> 01:38:24.873
You can chase them
all you want,

1615
01:38:24.953 --> 01:38:27.431
you're just gonna wind up
holding your own tails.

1616
01:38:28.430 --> 01:38:31.237
Or, you can let them
come to you.

1617
01:38:31.307 --> 01:38:32.875
So, what's it gonna be?

1618
01:38:32.985 --> 01:38:34.110
Chase them?

1619
01:38:35.103 --> 01:38:36.467
Or make a deal with me?

1620
01:38:42.935 --> 01:38:44.743
Again? How did we
get found out again?

1621
01:38:44.813 --> 01:38:46.628
It's fine, it's fine.
We'll just cut to the finale.

1622
01:38:46.652 --> 01:38:48.186
- Where's Merritt?
- Fine. I don't know.

1623
01:38:48.210 --> 01:38:49.210
I'm here.

1624
01:38:49.289 --> 01:38:51.096
- Where's Lula?
- I'm right here!

1625
01:38:51.167 --> 01:38:53.054
What the hell happened?

1626
01:38:53.125 --> 01:38:55.059
- My goddamn brother happened.
- Don't worry about it.

1627
01:38:55.083 --> 01:38:56.572
We just gotta get to the destination.

1628
01:38:56.642 --> 01:38:58.616
- Lula, can you handle that thing?
- I can ride it.

1629
01:38:58.640 --> 01:39:00.640
Have you asked the guys
that question, or just me?

1630
01:39:00.718 --> 01:39:01.956
- Yeah, good point.
- Let's go!

1631
01:39:02.036 --> 01:39:03.116
No, Jack.

1632
01:39:07.791 --> 01:39:08.791
Shit!

1633
01:39:11.747 --> 01:39:13.225
Jack's down. We gotta get him.

1634
01:39:16.542 --> 01:39:17.588
Come on, move!

1635
01:39:23.055 --> 01:39:25.294
- Jack! Go, go!
- That's the brake.

1636
01:39:25.373 --> 01:39:26.975
That's the gas.
You'll figure it out.

1637
01:39:27.051 --> 01:39:28.734
Come on, follow me!

1638
01:39:30.008 --> 01:39:31.008
That's it?

1639
01:39:47.631 --> 01:39:49.233
- Hey, ready?
- Yeah!

1640
01:39:56.062 --> 01:39:57.392
Get on.

1641
01:40:01.217 --> 01:40:03.343
- Yeah!
- Okay, guys, I'm coming!

1642
01:40:03.415 --> 01:40:05.734
Whoa! Jesus!

1643
01:40:07.171 --> 01:40:08.933
I'm coming!

1644
01:40:17.041 --> 01:40:18.882
- Get up! Come on!
- Okay.

1645
01:40:20.278 --> 01:40:21.483
You got me.

1646
01:40:21.557 --> 01:40:23.717
Those things are really dangerous!

1647
01:40:25.473 --> 01:40:27.678
- Come on!
- Go!

1648
01:40:29.469 --> 01:40:31.629
Get him in! Move it!

1649
01:40:36.062 --> 01:40:37.711
What is happening?

1650
01:40:37.820 --> 01:40:40.059
Where are they?

1651
01:40:40.138 --> 01:40:42.138
They should be
arriving any second.

1652
01:40:43.415 --> 01:40:45.858
That is, if they're gonna
get here by midnight.

1653
01:40:55.043 --> 01:40:56.930
Ah!

1654
01:40:58.120 --> 01:41:00.519
Okay, feeling fear.

1655
01:41:00.877 --> 01:41:03.560
Fear. Anger, anger.

1656
01:41:04.274 --> 01:41:07.275
- Oh, full house!
- That's enough.

1657
01:41:08.869 --> 01:41:10.279
I was done.

1658
01:41:11.227 --> 01:41:15.308
So nice to see you all again.

1659
01:41:15.383 --> 01:41:20.907
But since we're such old friends,
let's skip the formalities.

1660
01:41:21.896 --> 01:41:26.659
One of you
has something we require.

1661
01:41:28.290 --> 01:41:29.938
So give it over.

1662
01:41:38.120 --> 01:41:40.405
Nothing at all?

1663
01:41:41.637 --> 01:41:44.080
Really?

1664
01:41:45.872 --> 01:41:47.282
Sit down!

1665
01:41:54.464 --> 01:41:55.907
Let's go, let's go!

1666
01:41:57.141 --> 01:41:58.471
Let's go, let's go!

1667
01:41:58.540 --> 01:42:01.143
Hurry up and move them quickly
before they can do anything.

1668
01:42:01.217 --> 01:42:02.377
Get those lights off me!

1669
01:42:08.170 --> 01:42:09.739
Get on the plane!

1670
01:42:11.767 --> 01:42:13.926
Ow! Hey!

1671
01:42:16.562 --> 01:42:17.601
Get on the plane!

1672
01:42:27.191 --> 01:42:28.430
- Hey!
- Whoa, whoa, hey!

1673
01:42:28.510 --> 01:42:30.397
Yeah, uh, take off immediately.

1674
01:42:33.785 --> 01:42:36.433
You know, you make it almost too easy?

1675
01:42:36.502 --> 01:42:40.184
I mean, even your hilarious attempts
to make yourselves unpredictable,

1676
01:42:40.258 --> 01:42:42.099
just follow the same pattern each time.

1677
01:42:42.176 --> 01:42:44.176
Set up, set up, set up.

1678
01:42:44.254 --> 01:42:45.493
Big reveal.

1679
01:42:45.573 --> 01:42:47.971
Which tonight was going
to be stroke of midnight,

1680
01:42:48.050 --> 01:42:50.779
middle of the Thames?
Tell me if I'm warm.

1681
01:42:55.683 --> 01:42:59.479
Well, there will be a big reveal,
and it will come now.

1682
01:43:01.237 --> 01:43:04.317
The stick, dear boy.

1683
01:43:04.873 --> 01:43:06.158
- No.
- Fine.

1684
01:43:06.712 --> 01:43:08.916
Start with her.

1685
01:43:08.989 --> 01:43:11.308
Whoa, whoa, hey, hey, hey!

1686
01:43:13.505 --> 01:43:16.903
- Give it to him.
- Danny, give him the card.

1687
01:43:16.981 --> 01:43:18.822
- Just give it to him.
- Danny, come on.

1688
01:43:28.849 --> 01:43:29.974
We'll have a closer look.

1689
01:43:38.440 --> 01:43:40.406
Okay, I owe you an apology.

1690
01:43:40.478 --> 01:43:42.763
Leader, you should have
planned an escape, okay?

1691
01:43:42.835 --> 01:43:44.490
- This is the both of us.
- Okay, it's the both of us.

1692
01:43:44.514 --> 01:43:46.002
- Guys, come on!
- Come on, stop!

1693
01:43:46.072 --> 01:43:48.879
- You, to save your little girlfriend!
- To save my...

1694
01:43:48.949 --> 01:43:51.234
- He had a knife to her head!
- I'm a Horseman, you dick!

1695
01:43:51.307 --> 01:43:52.801
All right, you know what?
This is not the time!

1696
01:43:52.825 --> 01:43:54.269
And you, what's your deal, man?

1697
01:43:54.344 --> 01:43:56.470
Look, they're not gonna get blood
all over the seats.

1698
01:43:56.542 --> 01:43:58.236
They're probably gonna
throw us out of the plane.

1699
01:43:58.260 --> 01:43:59.595
- Really?
- I'm sorry to interrupt,

1700
01:43:59.619 --> 01:44:01.745
but I just have to
say congratulations.

1701
01:44:01.816 --> 01:44:03.021
And thank you!

1702
01:44:04.574 --> 01:44:05.812
It's real.

1703
01:44:05.892 --> 01:44:07.302
What?

1704
01:44:07.371 --> 01:44:08.894
It's real?

1705
01:44:10.608 --> 01:44:11.654
Bring them through.

1706
01:44:11.727 --> 01:44:12.806
Did you say it's real?

1707
01:44:12.885 --> 01:44:13.940
What is he talking about?

1708
01:44:13.964 --> 01:44:15.169
Move!

1709
01:44:15.243 --> 01:44:16.767
Well done, my boy.

1710
01:44:20.638 --> 01:44:23.036
Cote Du Marisule champagne.

1711
01:44:23.115 --> 01:44:27.241
It's the most expensive bottle on Earth.

1712
01:44:28.150 --> 01:44:30.276
$1.2 million.

1713
01:44:30.348 --> 01:44:34.349
And I saved it just for this occasion.

1714
01:44:34.424 --> 01:44:38.664
Honestly, same bottle,
Liquor Shack, 700K.

1715
01:44:38.740 --> 01:44:41.865
Hey. You got what you wanted.

1716
01:44:41.936 --> 01:44:44.460
So, does this mean
you're letting us go?

1717
01:44:44.534 --> 01:44:47.455
Knowing what you
know about us? No.

1718
01:44:47.531 --> 01:44:51.213
- You sure?
- Chase, what shall we do?

1719
01:44:53.005 --> 01:44:55.165
Toss them out.

1720
01:44:55.243 --> 01:44:57.811
Get your last words
in quickly, everybody!

1721
01:44:59.759 --> 01:45:03.202
- No! Come on! No!
- Farewell.

1722
01:45:03.555 --> 01:45:05.760
Oh, God! No!

1723
01:45:07.031 --> 01:45:09.430
Wait! I just wanna say...

1724
01:45:09.509 --> 01:45:11.748
I came into the world
with the wrong family,

1725
01:45:11.826 --> 01:45:13.793
but I'm leaving with the right one.

1726
01:45:13.864 --> 01:45:15.433
I love you guys!

1727
01:45:15.503 --> 01:45:16.549
- No!
- No, no, no!

1728
01:45:18.380 --> 01:45:20.823
This is how I kicked
you out of the womb.

1729
01:45:20.897 --> 01:45:21.977
No!

1730
01:45:23.495 --> 01:45:24.858
Who's next?

1731
01:45:31.007 --> 01:45:32.848
No, wait! Let me go!

1732
01:45:32.925 --> 01:45:35.244
Let go of me!

1733
01:45:35.323 --> 01:45:37.642
No, no, no!

1734
01:45:37.721 --> 01:45:39.403
- Let me go!
- Toss her!

1735
01:45:41.797 --> 01:45:44.718
Oh, God!

1736
01:45:44.794 --> 01:45:47.795
Hurry up and shut that door!
It's getting cold in here.

1737
01:45:47.870 --> 01:45:49.792
Okay, hit the road, Jack!

1738
01:45:55.423 --> 01:45:56.548
No, no, no!

1739
01:45:57.980 --> 01:46:00.219
A lot of brain,
but not enough brawn.

1740
01:46:04.014 --> 01:46:05.014
Oh, that was fun!

1741
01:46:07.890 --> 01:46:10.891
Cheers! Well done, my boy.

1742
01:46:15.403 --> 01:46:16.733
Um...

1743
01:46:16.801 --> 01:46:18.961
No, is it supposed to taste like that?

1744
01:46:19.079 --> 01:46:20.568
No, it is not.

1745
01:46:40.378 --> 01:46:41.946
What the hell is going on?

1746
01:46:51.926 --> 01:46:54.165
Okay, come pick them up!

1747
01:46:54.883 --> 01:46:56.611
- Come on.
- Come on out.

1748
01:47:09.868 --> 01:47:13.711
<i>Ladies and gentlemen... The Horsemen!</i>

1749
01:47:34.044 --> 01:47:36.283
<i>No, you should feel pretty
good about yourself, man.</i>

1750
01:47:36.402 --> 01:47:39.925
<i>You predicted it correctly.
The Thames, stroke of midnight.</i>

1751
01:47:39.998 --> 01:47:41.805
<i>New Year's Eve. Happy New Year!</i>

1752
01:47:48.829 --> 01:47:50.114
Unbelievable!

1753
01:47:50.188 --> 01:47:51.563
- We are moving now.
- The whole thing just changed.

1754
01:47:51.587 --> 01:47:54.349
- All backup into position stat!
- We're on the move.

1755
01:47:55.822 --> 01:47:58.061
- You're staying here!
- Of course.

1756
01:48:05.413 --> 01:48:07.254
Move! Move! Out of the way!

1757
01:48:07.331 --> 01:48:09.570
Wow! Thank you very much, London!

1758
01:48:09.649 --> 01:48:13.933
We would like to acknowledge not just
our old friend, Arthur Tressler.

1759
01:48:14.004 --> 01:48:16.573
But his young and very
brilliant son, Walter Mabry.

1760
01:48:16.642 --> 01:48:19.040
Who has performed one of the
greatest feats of illusion

1761
01:48:19.119 --> 01:48:20.768
even we have ever seen.

1762
01:48:20.837 --> 01:48:25.043
He has, amazingly, brought
himself back from the dead!

1763
01:48:25.153 --> 01:48:27.757
But before he did that,

1764
01:48:27.830 --> 01:48:29.194
he revealed someone.

1765
01:48:29.269 --> 01:48:34.395
And we think it only right
to give him a proper introduction.

1766
01:48:34.464 --> 01:48:38.465
He is our friend and he is our leader.
Dylan Shrike!

1767
01:48:38.540 --> 01:48:40.540
- Get over here!
- Yeah!

1768
01:48:46.412 --> 01:48:48.458
So, normally

1769
01:48:48.570 --> 01:48:53.253
only the magician, his assistant,
and a few trusted stage hands

1770
01:48:53.325 --> 01:48:55.564
know the secret of a trick.

1771
01:48:55.643 --> 01:48:58.644
But tonight, however, in this effect,

1772
01:48:58.720 --> 01:49:01.800
all of you people
around the Thames here.

1773
01:49:01.876 --> 01:49:06.514
And since we're streaming live,
everyone around the world

1774
01:49:06.592 --> 01:49:09.115
already knows everything.

1775
01:49:09.189 --> 01:49:13.270
And only these fellows
here are the ones left in the dark.

1776
01:49:13.345 --> 01:49:16.266
So let's see how closely
you were watching our shows.

1777
01:49:16.342 --> 01:49:20.059
Remember when we were playing
"Find the Lady," and I said

1778
01:49:20.138 --> 01:49:22.059
<i>that we used a Barry Trap.</i>

1779
01:49:22.855 --> 01:49:26.413
<i>Some classic switches
and a few duplicates?</i>

1780
01:49:26.492 --> 01:49:30.015
<i>Well, that's how we switched
the driver of the truck.</i>

1781
01:49:33.085 --> 01:49:35.324
<i>Which took us to a duplicate hangar</i>

1782
01:49:35.443 --> 01:49:37.966
<i>where we used your need to rush,</i>

1783
01:49:38.040 --> 01:49:41.041
<i>and your fear of being seen
let you do the work for us.</i>

1784
01:49:41.117 --> 01:49:42.924
Get those lights off me!

1785
01:49:44.234 --> 01:49:48.474
So, the airplane.
How did we get it to fly?

1786
01:49:49.549 --> 01:49:54.345
<i>Well, we used fans,
and lights and rain machines.</i>

1787
01:49:59.579 --> 01:50:04.216
Obviously, all of this was
reliant on us getting caught.

1788
01:50:04.294 --> 01:50:05.579
But we needed to make sure

1789
01:50:05.693 --> 01:50:07.773
that you would do exactly
what we needed you to do

1790
01:50:07.850 --> 01:50:09.339
once you did catch us.

1791
01:50:09.409 --> 01:50:15.490
My brother Chase, who was
technical adviser to the losing team.

1792
01:50:15.563 --> 01:50:18.211
He taught us that it's
all about blind spots.

1793
01:50:18.320 --> 01:50:19.843
I know where you're going!

1794
01:50:19.918 --> 01:50:23.522
And, uh, surprises.

1795
01:50:25.353 --> 01:50:26.876
Watch it, hobo.

1796
01:50:26.951 --> 01:50:28.634
Why don't you watch
the watch I'm wearing,

1797
01:50:28.710 --> 01:50:31.358
the where of where
you're watching, and sleep!

1798
01:50:31.427 --> 01:50:34.746
It's going to be your idea to throw
the Horsemen out of the plane.

1799
01:50:34.823 --> 01:50:37.301
If all of this is
kind of confusing to you,

1800
01:50:37.381 --> 01:50:39.347
I can maybe catch you up on it later.

1801
01:50:39.419 --> 01:50:41.862
Probably see you what, three to five?

1802
01:50:43.015 --> 01:50:47.380
Come on, put it there.
Hey! Tit for tat, bro!

1803
01:50:47.930 --> 01:50:50.897
Basically, we showed them everything.

1804
01:50:50.967 --> 01:50:56.253
How you kidnapped us to steal the very
thing that is in your pocket right now.

1805
01:50:58.480 --> 01:50:59.843
Whoops!

1806
01:51:03.475 --> 01:51:05.918
You know, this thing here,

1807
01:51:05.992 --> 01:51:09.471
which you said you could
use to adjust markets,

1808
01:51:09.549 --> 01:51:13.231
manipulate governments,
and spy on whomever you choose.

1809
01:51:13.305 --> 01:51:15.146
Also, you could, as you said,

1810
01:51:15.223 --> 01:51:18.383
control the public
from outside the grid.

1811
01:51:19.299 --> 01:51:24.585
These men destroy people's lives.

1812
01:51:24.694 --> 01:51:28.740
Spying on the world, robbing you
of your right to privacy.

1813
01:51:28.809 --> 01:51:32.049
And they do that
by hiding in the dark.

1814
01:51:32.126 --> 01:51:36.684
So, in the Horsemen tradition,
we're here to expose them.

1815
01:51:36.762 --> 01:51:41.797
Tonight, they, like all of us,

1816
01:51:41.876 --> 01:51:45.036
are finally stepping into the light.

1817
01:51:48.989 --> 01:51:50.194
Thank you, everybody!

1818
01:51:50.268 --> 01:51:52.871
We are the Horsemen
and we will be back very soon!

1819
01:51:55.183 --> 01:51:59.184
Five, four, three, two, one!

1820
01:52:16.002 --> 01:52:18.606
- Hey, we're on the clock.
- Get a move on.

1821
01:52:21.677 --> 01:52:22.801
There they are!

1822
01:52:22.875 --> 01:52:24.921
- There's more inside.
- Don't you bloody touch me!

1823
01:52:24.993 --> 01:52:26.676
The Horsemen are over on the...

1824
01:52:29.469 --> 01:52:30.912
Oh, shit!

1825
01:52:31.587 --> 01:52:33.394
Stay with them!

1826
01:52:33.465 --> 01:52:35.465
Everybody spread out!

1827
01:52:35.543 --> 01:52:37.384
Nobody leaves this barge!

1828
01:52:39.299 --> 01:52:42.379
Rhodes, hold it right there!
Do not move!

1829
01:52:48.050 --> 01:52:49.733
This contains everything.

1830
01:52:49.808 --> 01:52:52.888
Not just Walter and Tressler,
but all their dealings.

1831
01:52:52.965 --> 01:52:55.966
All their contacts,
the entire network.

1832
01:52:56.042 --> 01:52:58.247
And why on Earth
would I believe you?

1833
01:52:58.360 --> 01:53:01.997
I told you.
I'm the same man I've always been.

1834
01:53:02.076 --> 01:53:04.554
Move it! Come on!
Let's go, go, go!

1835
01:53:04.634 --> 01:53:07.441
I'm not part of this
organization! I'm Merritt.

1836
01:53:07.511 --> 01:53:10.717
I'm a patsy!
I'm a patsy!

1837
01:53:11.627 --> 01:53:13.514
Ten minutes.

1838
01:53:15.303 --> 01:53:17.349
Now you're playing
the long game.

1839
01:53:31.207 --> 01:53:33.446
Dad, I promise you I will fix this.

1840
01:53:33.525 --> 01:53:35.889
You can't. Stop calling me dad.

1841
01:53:35.962 --> 01:53:39.883
There were so many women,
I have no idea whose bastard you are.

1842
01:53:39.958 --> 01:53:41.083
What?

1843
01:53:41.157 --> 01:53:43.522
- What happened?
- Thin air, gone. Come with me.

1844
01:53:43.595 --> 01:53:47.232
What're you talking about, gone?
People don't just disappear.

1845
01:53:47.311 --> 01:53:50.039
You realize that he doesn't actually
have magical powers!

1846
01:54:29.669 --> 01:54:31.112
- Hey, guys.
- Hey!

1847
01:54:31.187 --> 01:54:33.586
- Good to see you all.
- Good to see you, Li.

1848
01:54:34.704 --> 01:54:35.828
Come on in.

1849
01:54:39.099 --> 01:54:41.940
Good morning. Welcome.

1850
01:54:44.374 --> 01:54:47.931
I'm proud of you.
All of you.

1851
01:54:48.010 --> 01:54:51.534
I have a lot to show you. Come.

1852
01:54:52.766 --> 01:54:54.004
Come.

1853
01:55:05.353 --> 01:55:06.558
Dad.

1854
01:55:23.974 --> 01:55:25.577
Yes.

1855
01:55:25.693 --> 01:55:28.421
Your father and I were partners.

1856
01:55:28.490 --> 01:55:31.650
Our act was that of rivals.

1857
01:55:32.845 --> 01:55:38.483
I was the hard-eyed realist.
He was the dreamy-eyed idealist.

1858
01:55:38.560 --> 01:55:41.288
We thought it was the perfect misdirect.

1859
01:55:42.356 --> 01:55:45.993
Yes, in public, I did goad him.

1860
01:55:46.072 --> 01:55:50.914
The only thing we didn't count on
was it going so wrong.

1861
01:55:54.943 --> 01:55:56.751
Why?

1862
01:55:56.861 --> 01:55:58.589
Why didn't you ever say anything to me?

1863
01:55:58.660 --> 01:56:00.183
I don't know.

1864
01:56:01.337 --> 01:56:02.622
Give it a name.

1865
01:56:02.736 --> 01:56:06.134
Shame, guilt. Regret.

1866
01:56:09.888 --> 01:56:12.855
Hey, guys, uh, you might
wanna come take a look at this.

1867
01:56:14.524 --> 01:56:15.570
Hmm.

1868
01:56:17.041 --> 01:56:18.246
Look.

1869
01:56:18.360 --> 01:56:20.122
<i>I put you in jail, man.</i>

1870
01:56:20.198 --> 01:56:22.563
Why didn't you
say something then?

1871
01:56:22.636 --> 01:56:25.637
Well, you know, I was going to.

1872
01:56:25.713 --> 01:56:30.350
But then, you were so quick to show
your personal motivations that...

1873
01:56:31.627 --> 01:56:35.025
Well, quite frankly
it spoiled the trick for me.

1874
01:56:35.103 --> 01:56:36.388
I showed my weakness.

1875
01:56:36.462 --> 01:56:39.269
Well, it showed you weren't ready.

1876
01:56:39.339 --> 01:56:41.987
But when you came back to my cell,

1877
01:56:42.056 --> 01:56:46.455
you had let go of your
30-year vendetta against me...

1878
01:56:47.451 --> 01:56:48.939
...for your Horsemen.

1879
01:56:50.528 --> 01:56:55.211
<i>And I said to myself,
"That's Lionel's boy."</i>

1880
01:56:56.082 --> 01:56:59.481
<i>And I knew it was my job to see to it</i>

1881
01:56:59.559 --> 01:57:03.276
<i>that he became the magician
he was born to be.</i>

1882
01:57:03.355 --> 01:57:05.674
All that was left then was...

1883
01:57:07.431 --> 01:57:08.761
For me to hit bottom.

1884
01:57:09.549 --> 01:57:14.391
So the safe, my father's wristwatch,
that was all you?

1885
01:57:17.181 --> 01:57:20.500
<i>Walter and Tressler,
were they in on it too?</i>

1886
01:57:20.578 --> 01:57:23.419
<i>Oh, we knew
Walter was in hiding.</i>

1887
01:57:23.495 --> 01:57:27.212
<i>The Octa show was a setup
to expose him to the public.</i>

1888
01:57:28.929 --> 01:57:32.373
<i>And you were right
about the Horsemen.</i>

1889
01:57:32.486 --> 01:57:37.521
<i>They needed to learn to work together
like a single organism.</i>

1890
01:57:45.193 --> 01:57:47.797
Now I know why
you look so familiar.

1891
01:57:47.870 --> 01:57:49.473
You're the Horsemen, aren't you?

1892
01:57:57.181 --> 01:57:58.591
Okay.

1893
01:57:58.660 --> 01:58:00.706
<i>But that can't be
what all this is about.</i>

1894
01:58:00.817 --> 01:58:05.296
You know, the best tricks
work on many levels.

1895
01:58:05.373 --> 01:58:09.340
And the deepest one,
for me, at least,

1896
01:58:09.409 --> 01:58:12.216
is this moment right here.

1897
01:58:15.842 --> 01:58:17.366
I'm sorry, Dylan.

1898
01:58:18.000 --> 01:58:20.524
I miss your father.

1899
01:58:20.598 --> 01:58:24.076
Thirty years is a long time to drag
something around, isn't it?

1900
01:58:24.194 --> 01:58:25.842
Yes, it is.

1901
01:58:35.783 --> 01:58:37.067
So, now what?

1902
01:58:39.499 --> 01:58:42.386
- Now I'm about out of moves.
- Oh...

1903
01:58:42.456 --> 01:58:43.819
And I'm tired.

1904
01:58:43.894 --> 01:58:46.268
Come on, you're the one who said
that you believe that this road

1905
01:58:46.292 --> 01:58:48.258
led to the great wizard
behind the curtain.

1906
01:58:48.330 --> 01:58:49.376
I did.

1907
01:58:49.449 --> 01:58:51.609
And that you'd finally
get to look him in the eyes.

1908
01:58:53.005 --> 01:58:54.688
I am.

1909
01:59:00.478 --> 01:59:02.921
It's your game now.

1910
01:59:02.995 --> 01:59:05.599
You play it however you see fit.

1911
01:59:06.831 --> 01:59:12.674
My suggestion, however, is that
you find yourself a successor.

1912
01:59:12.746 --> 01:59:14.633
Hi. Okay, so, um...

1913
01:59:14.704 --> 01:59:18.022
Well, obviously,
we have a few questions. Right?

1914
01:59:18.100 --> 01:59:20.578
Uh... By "a few,"
I think he means, like, five million.

1915
01:59:20.658 --> 01:59:22.226
- Yeah.
- Well.

1916
01:59:22.296 --> 01:59:24.137
Good luck to you all.

1917
01:59:24.214 --> 01:59:25.862
- Okay. See, that's not fair.
- What!

1918
01:59:25.932 --> 01:59:27.067
- Come on.
- That's not right.

1919
01:59:27.091 --> 01:59:28.426
That's gonna make me really angry!

1920
01:59:28.450 --> 01:59:29.705
Not right now.

1921
01:59:29.729 --> 01:59:31.331
Oh, um, by the way...

1922
01:59:33.565 --> 01:59:36.042
Pay no attention to the curtain.

1923
01:59:38.959 --> 01:59:40.323
There is a curtain.

1924
01:59:40.398 --> 01:59:41.728
Go, go, go!

1925
01:59:51.107 --> 01:59:55.552
<i>Seeing is believing.
But is it truth?</i>

1926
01:59:55.623 --> 01:59:58.271
<i>Depends on your point of view.</i>

1927
01:59:58.340 --> 02:00:00.863
<i>Are you listening, Horsemen?</i>

1928
02:00:00.937 --> 02:00:05.257
<i>When you emerge, and you will,</i>

1929
02:00:05.333 --> 02:00:08.459
<i>I will be there waiting.</i>

1930
02:00:08.570 --> 02:00:14.128
<i>Because mark my words,
you will get what's coming to you</i>

1931
02:00:14.204 --> 02:00:16.852
<i>in ways you can't expect.</i>

1932
02:00:17.721 --> 02:00:20.403
<i>But very much deserve.</i>

1933
02:00:20.478 --> 02:00:23.604
<i>Because one thing I believe in</i>

1934
02:00:23.675 --> 02:00:27.562
<i>is an eye for an eye.</i>
